const OFFICIAL_QUESTIONS = [
  {id:1,element:1,q:"A federally regulated trust company seeks to trade interest rate swaps to manage portfolio risk. What is the Investment Dealer's responsibility for suitability assessment before processing the trade?",options:{A:"Assess the client's sophistication to determine suitability requirements",B:"Ensure the client understands the risks of interest rate swaps",C:"Exempt the client from trade-specific suitability determinations",D:"Evaluate the client's reliance on the Investment Dealer's recommendations"},answer:"C",explanation:"Federally regulated trust companies are institutional clients. Investment Dealers are exempt from trade-specific suitability determinations for institutional clients — the firm does not assess each individual trade for suitability.",wrongExplanations:{A:"This describes a suitability step for determining institutional status, but the correct action is exemption from trade-specific suitability — not a separate sophistication assessment.",B:"Ensuring understanding is good practice but it is not the regulatory obligation here. Institutional clients are exempt from the full suitability process.",D:"Evaluating reliance on recommendations applies to retail client suitability, not to institutional clients like federally regulated trust companies."}},
  {id:2,element:4,q:"Which term best describes an approach that combines commitment-of-traders reports with a time series momentum signal to forecast soybean futures prices?",options:{A:"Quantitative high-frequency model",B:"Blended fundamental-technical method",C:"Pure sentiment-driven technique",D:"Volatility term-structure model"},answer:"B",explanation:"Commitment-of-traders reports are a fundamental/sentiment data source; time series momentum is a technical signal. Combining both = a blended fundamental-technical method.",wrongExplanations:{A:"High-frequency models focus on microsecond price data, not on macro reports like COT data.",C:"While COT reports do reflect market sentiment, momentum signals are technical — so 'pure sentiment' is inaccurate.",D:"Volatility term-structure models look at implied volatility across different expiry dates, not at COT or momentum data."}},
  {id:3,element:6,q:"An institutional bondholder with a $10 million portfolio of 10-year government bonds (duration 8.4 years) wants to hedge against rising interest rates using bond futures (duration 7.2 years, notional $100,000). Which hedging strategy best hedges this position?",options:{A:"Short 86 bond futures",B:"Long 86 bond futures",C:"Short 117 bond futures",D:"Long 117 bond futures"},answer:"C",explanation:"Contracts = (Portfolio Value × Portfolio Duration) / (Futures Duration × Futures Notional) = ($10,000,000 × 8.4) / (7.2 × $100,000) = 116.67 ≈ 117. Short futures to hedge against rising rates (which reduce bond prices).",wrongExplanations:{A:"86 contracts uses incorrect duration math. The correct calculation yields approximately 117 contracts.",B:"Going LONG futures would add duration exposure, not hedge it. Rising rates would hurt both the bond portfolio and the long futures.",D:"117 is the right number but going LONG is wrong — to hedge a long bond position against rising rates, you must go SHORT futures."}},
  {id:4,element:5,q:"A block trade for futures is executed off-exchange. What settlement aspect differs from standard trades?",options:{A:"Margin calculation for position sizing",B:"Reporting deadlines to the exchange system",C:"Currency conversion for settlement payments",D:"Clearing house guarantee for trade security"},answer:"B",explanation:"Block trades are negotiated off-exchange but must still be reported to the exchange within specified deadlines. The reporting deadline differs from standard on-exchange trades, though clearing house guarantees and margin rules still apply.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Margin calculations follow the same rules for block trades — the exchange margin requirements still apply once reported.",C:"Currency conversion processes are the same regardless of whether a trade is a block or standard exchange trade.",D:"Once reported, block trades are cleared by the clearing house like standard trades — the guarantee still applies."}},
  {id:5,element:6,q:"A Registered Representative must hedge a 250,000-share long position in a $20 mid-cap stock. The at-the-money call option has a delta of 0.50 and covers 100 shares. How many call-option contracts must be written to make the position delta-neutral?",options:{A:"2,500",B:"5,000",C:"7,500",D:"10,000"},answer:"B",explanation:"Contracts = Shares / (Delta × Shares per contract) = 250,000 / (0.50 × 100) = 5,000 contracts. Writing (shorting) 5,000 call contracts creates a negative delta of 250,000 × 0.50 to offset the long stock delta.",wrongExplanations:{A:"2,500 only gives a delta offset of 125,000 (half the required), leaving 125,000 shares still unhedged.",C:"7,500 over-hedges the position, creating a net short delta of 125,000 — introducing directional risk.",D:"10,000 contracts would double-hedge the position, creating a large unintended short exposure."}},
  {id:6,element:1,q:"A Registered Representative becomes aware that a client has changed jobs and is now earning significantly more. What should the RR do to ensure the client's records remain compliant?",options:{A:"Wait for the client to initiate any necessary changes before updating the file",B:"Note the change informally and include it at the next annual review",C:"Update the client's profile and confirm with the client before initiating the next transaction",D:"Consult legal before making changes to an active account"},answer:"C",explanation:"KYC information must be kept current. When material changes occur (like a significant income change), the RR must update the profile promptly and confirm it with the client before placing the next trade.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Waiting for the client to initiate changes is incorrect — the RR has an active duty to keep records current when they become aware of material changes.",B:"Informal notes and deferring to an annual review is insufficient. A material change to financial situation must be addressed before the next transaction.",D:"Consulting legal is not the required step here. This is a standard KYC update obligation that the RR handles directly."}},
  {id:7,element:3,q:"An investor enters a long forward contract at $500 when the underlying asset is trading at $495. At contract expiry, the market price is $520. The contract value is $10 per $0.01. What is the investor's profit?",options:{A:"$200",B:"$250",C:"$20,000",D:"$25,000"},answer:"C",explanation:"Price moved from $500 (entry) to $520 (exit) = $20 gain. Contract value = $10 per $0.01 = $1,000 per $1.00 move. Profit = $20 × $1,000 = $20,000.",wrongExplanations:{A:"$200 incorrectly ignores the contract multiplier. You must multiply the price gain by the contract value.",B:"$250 may reflect the $5 difference between spot and futures at entry but ignores the multiplier and the correct exit price.",D:"$25,000 incorrectly uses the move from spot ($495) to exit ($520) = $25 × $1,000. The correct starting point is the forward contract price of $500, not the spot."}},
  {id:8,element:3,q:"An investor purchases a call option on 100 shares of a stock for a premium of $10 per share, with a strike price of $140 per share. The stock's current price is $150 per share. What is the leverage ratio of this option position?",options:{A:"1.07:1 ratio, based on stock price and strike price",B:"14:1 ratio, based on strike price and premium",C:"15:1 ratio, based on market value and cost",D:"100:1 ratio, based on the number of shares per option"},answer:"C",explanation:"Leverage = Market Value of underlying controlled / Cost of option = (100 × $150) / (100 × $10) = $15,000 / $1,000 = 15:1.",wrongExplanations:{A:"1.07:1 is just the ratio of stock price to strike ($150/$140). This is not the leverage ratio.",B:"14:1 uses the strike price instead of the market price. Leverage uses the current market value of the controlled asset.",D:"100:1 confuses the number of shares in a contract (100) with the leverage ratio. These are unrelated figures."}},
  {id:9,element:3,q:"An exchange-traded deep out-of-the-money (OTM) put option trades infrequently with wide bid-ask spreads. Which risk is most acute for the holder?",options:{A:"The option may be exercised leading to a loss",B:"The seller may default on the obligation",C:"Significant price concessions when exiting",D:"Unexpected margin calls due to liquidity"},answer:"C",explanation:"Wide bid-ask spreads and infrequent trading = liquidity risk. The holder may have to accept a much worse price than fair value when trying to sell the position (exit). This is the most acute risk given the described market conditions.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Exercise risk is not a concern for option BUYERS — the holder has the right but not the obligation to exercise. They would simply not exercise an OTM option.",B:"For exchange-traded options, the clearing house guarantees performance — seller default is minimal.",D:"Option BUYERS are not subject to margin calls (they pay the premium upfront). Margin calls affect short option positions."}},
  {id:10,element:3,q:"A company enters into a plain vanilla interest rate swap to pay a fixed rate of 3.5% and receive a floating rate tied to SOFR on a $10 million notional amount. If SOFR is 2.8% during the first semi-annual payment period, what is the net payment obligation for the company?",options:{A:"$35,000 payment to the counterparty",B:"$17,500 payment to the counterparty",C:"$35,000 receipt from the counterparty",D:"$17,500 receipt from the counterparty"},answer:"A",explanation:"Fixed payment = $10M × 3.5% × 0.5 = $175,000. Floating receipt = $10M × 2.8% × 0.5 = $140,000. Net = pays $35,000 to counterparty. The company pays more fixed than it receives floating.",wrongExplanations:{B:"$17,500 ignores the semi-annual factor (0.5) on one side. Both fixed and floating are semi-annual payments.",C:"The company receives the LOWER floating rate (2.8%) and pays the HIGHER fixed rate (3.5%), so they make a net payment — not a receipt.",D:"$17,500 receipt is doubly wrong — wrong direction (should be paying) and wrong amount."}},
  {id:11,element:1,q:"An accredited investor messages the Registered Representative on a personal messaging app asking whether to short more bitcoin futures. Under CIRO rules on client communications, how should the RR respond?",options:{A:"Move the chat to the firm's monitored app before giving guidance",B:"Reply on the same app, then email compliance a screenshot for archiving",C:"Wait until the client phones the approved office line",D:"Answer on the same app but restrict comments to public market headlines"},answer:"A",explanation:"CIRO rules prohibit off-channel communications. The RR must move the conversation to a firm-approved, monitored platform before providing any investment guidance.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Emailing a screenshot after the fact does not satisfy the requirement. The communication must occur on an approved channel, not be retroactively archived.",C:"Waiting for a phone call is not specifically required — any firm-approved monitored channel would be acceptable, not just the phone.",D:"The channel restriction applies regardless of content type. Even discussing public market data on a personal app violates off-channel communication rules."}},
  {id:12,element:3,q:"A Canadian company enters into a one-year currency swap with a UK counterparty: pays £500,000 annually, receives CAD 850,000 annually. Initial rate: 1.70 CAD/GBP; maturity rate: 1.65 CAD/GBP. Notional amounts are exchanged at start and end. What is the net gain or loss in CAD for the Canadian company due to the change in exchange rates?",options:{A:"Loss of CAD 25,000",B:"Gain of CAD 25,000",C:"Gain of CAD 50,000",D:"Loss of CAD 50,000"},answer:"B",explanation:"At start: pays CAD 850,000 and receives £500,000 (worth CAD 850,000 at 1.70). At maturity: returns £500,000 now worth only CAD 825,000 (at 1.65 rate). The company repays £500,000 that is now worth CAD 825,000 less than the CAD 850,000 received — a CAD 25,000 gain.",wrongExplanations:{A:"It is actually a GAIN, not a loss. The CAD strengthened vs GBP (lower CAD/GBP rate), so the company returns cheaper GBP than it received.",C:"CAD 50,000 would be correct if the rate moved by 0.10 (£500,000 × 0.10 = CAD 50,000), but the move was only 0.05.",D:"Loss of 50,000 is doubly wrong — wrong direction and wrong magnitude."}},
  {id:13,element:6,q:"A six-month gold futures price exceeds its fair value based on spot price and risk-free rate. What is the arbitrage strategy to exploit this mispricing?",options:{A:"Buy spot gold and sell gold futures",B:"Sell spot gold and buy gold futures",C:"Buy spot gold and sell it in six months",D:"Sell gold futures and hold for six months"},answer:"A",explanation:"Cash-and-carry arbitrage: if futures are overpriced vs fair value (spot + cost of carry), buy the underlying spot and simultaneously sell the overpriced futures. At expiry the positions converge and a risk-free profit is locked in.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Sell spot and buy futures is reverse cash-and-carry — used when futures are UNDERPRICED. Here futures are overpriced so this is the wrong direction.",C:"Buying spot and selling at market in 6 months is not arbitrage — it exposes you to price risk with no locked-in profit.",D:"Selling futures alone without buying spot does not lock in a risk-free gain — you are exposed to the spot price at expiry."}},
  {id:14,element:2,q:"A retail client holds an unexpired call option on a government bond futures contract. What information must be shown on the client's monthly account statement?",options:{A:"The value of the bond futures contract and the client's profit or loss on the option",B:"The amount of margin the client posted and any margin calls during the month",C:"The type of option, the strike price and the delivery date of the futures contract",D:"A disclosure of risks of leaving any future contract option expired"},answer:"C",explanation:"CIRO rules require monthly statements for options on futures to show the type of option (call/put), the strike price, and the delivery date of the underlying futures contract. These are the specific mandatory disclosures.",wrongExplanations:{A:"While account value is shown generally, the specific required disclosures for options on futures are type, strike, and delivery date — not P&L.",B:"Margin details are important but not the specific regulatory requirement for the options-on-futures position disclosure.",D:"Risk disclosures about expiring options are not a standard monthly statement requirement — the risk disclosure is provided at account opening."}},
  {id:15,element:5,q:"Which of the following accounts qualifies as a non-client account?",options:{A:"An individual investment account of a retail client",B:"An institutional client's managed futures account",C:"A trust account managed for a high-net-worth individual",D:"An Investment Dealer employee's personal trading account"},answer:"D",explanation:"Non-client (NC) accounts are accounts belonging to employees, partners, directors, or related persons of the Investment Dealer — not outside clients. An employee's personal trading account is classified as NC.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Individual retail accounts are Client (CL) accounts, not non-client accounts.",B:"Institutional client accounts are still client accounts — they receive a CL designation.",C:"Trust accounts managed for outside individuals are client accounts, not non-client accounts."}},
  {id:16,element:3,q:"A retail client wants to hold a cryptocurrency contract for difference (CFD) over a weekend exchange closure. Which weekend liquidity risk must the Registered Representative explain?",options:{A:"Clearing houses make margin calls over the weekend",B:"Weekend funding still accrues and may widen the trade's cost",C:"Stop-loss orders may fill only after reopen at worse prices",D:"Volatility limits freeze the CFD price until Monday's open"},answer:"C",explanation:"CFD markets may be closed or illiquid over weekends. Stop-loss orders can only fill at the first available price on Monday's open, which may be significantly worse than the stop level due to overnight gaps (gap risk).",wrongExplanations:{A:"Clearing houses do not make margin calls over weekends for CFDs — the clearing process follows market hours.",B:"Weekend funding (overnight financing) is a cost risk but is known in advance and quantifiable — it is not the key liquidity risk being described.",D:"CFD prices are not frozen by volatility limits. The market simply closes and re-opens, which is when gap risk occurs."}},
  {id:17,element:4,q:"A stock is $41.80. A nine-month European call with a $40 strike costs $5.60, while the matching put costs $3.90. The risk-free rate is 2% per year (continuously compounded). Which strategy extracts the put-call parity mispricing?",options:{A:"Buy the put, sell the call, buy the shares and borrow the present value of $40",B:"Sell the put, sell the shares and lend the present value of $40",C:"Sell the put, buy the call, short the shares and lend the present value of $40",D:"Buy the call, buy the shares and borrow the present value of $40"},answer:"C",explanation:"Check parity: C - P = S - PV(K). PV($40) at 2% for 9 months ≈ $39.40. RHS = $41.80 - $39.40 = $2.40. LHS = $5.60 - $3.90 = $1.70. LHS < RHS: call is cheap / put is expensive. Arbitrage: sell the put (overpriced), buy the call (cheap), short the shares (to hedge), lend PV(K).",wrongExplanations:{A:"This strategy buys the put (expensive) instead of selling it. If the put is overpriced, you should SELL it.",B:"Selling both the put AND the shares in the same direction creates unintended exposure rather than locking in a riskless profit.",D:"Buying the call AND the shares doubles the bullish exposure without offsetting the mispriced put — no arbitrage profit is locked in."}},
  {id:18,element:6,q:"Implied volatility on a technology ETF is at a one-year low. A Registered Representative expects the coming earnings season to trigger a sharp move in either direction. Which option strategy best aligns with that view?",options:{A:"Sell an at-the-money straddle",B:"Write an out-of-the-money call",C:"Buy an at-the-money straddle",D:"Purchase an out-of-the-money put"},answer:"C",explanation:"Expecting a large move in either direction + low implied volatility (cheap options) = buy a straddle. An ATM straddle (long call + long put, same strike) profits from a large move in either direction. Low IV means you are buying options when they are relatively cheap.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Selling a straddle profits when prices stay flat — the opposite of expecting a sharp move. Also poor timing given low IV.",B:"Writing an OTM call is bearish and directional — not aligned with an uncertain-direction large-move view.",D:"Buying an OTM put is directional (bearish only) — if the move is upward, this position loses the premium. A straddle covers both directions."}},
  {id:19,element:3,q:"A Registered Representative wants to hedge seasonal jet fuel costs for an international airline. Why is a swap a better choice than a future on the same asset?",options:{A:"It manages the price risk at multiple points in the future",B:"It is cash settled, allowing better cash flow management",C:"It provides tighter bid-ask spreads during volatile trading",D:"It is based on a relevant underlying asset offsetting price risk"},answer:"A",explanation:"Jet fuel costs are seasonal and recurring throughout the year. A swap covers multiple future payment dates in a single agreement — hedging price risk at many points simultaneously. A single futures contract only covers one expiry date.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Many futures contracts are also cash-settled. Settlement method is not the key advantage of swaps for multi-period hedging.",C:"Swaps are OTC and typically have WIDER bid-ask spreads than exchange-traded futures, not tighter.",D:"Futures on the same asset would also be based on the relevant underlying. This is not a distinguishing advantage of swaps."}},
  {id:20,element:2,q:"After several trades in the same day, a client of an Investment Dealer has moved beyond their credit limit. Which of the following actions would be a breach of regulatory requirement?",options:{A:"Addressing margin obligations of the client",B:"Permitting continued trading by the client",C:"Reporting excessive trading to regulators",D:"Suspending the client's trading account"},answer:"B",explanation:"CIRO rules prohibit allowing a client to trade beyond their credit or margin limits. Permitting continued trading once a credit limit is breached is a regulatory violation.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Addressing margin obligations (calling for more margin) is the CORRECT required action — not a breach.",C:"Reporting concerns to regulators is appropriate compliance behavior, not a breach.",D:"Suspending the account to prevent further trading beyond limits is a permitted and appropriate response."}},
  {id:21,element:4,q:"The spot exchange rate is 1.3500 CAD/USD. The one-year risk-free rate is 3% in Canada and 5% in the US. According to interest rate parity, what should the one-year forward exchange rate be?",options:{A:"1.32 CAD/USD",B:"1.34 CAD/USD",C:"1.35 CAD/USD",D:"1.38 CAD/USD"},answer:"A",explanation:"Forward rate = Spot × (1 + r_domestic) / (1 + r_foreign) = 1.3500 × (1.03 / 1.05) = 1.3500 × 0.9810 = 1.3243 ≈ 1.32 CAD/USD. Lower Canadian rates mean CAD should appreciate vs USD in the forward market.",wrongExplanations:{B:"1.34 is close but uses rounded or incorrect rate differentials.",C:"1.35 would mean no change — this ignores the interest rate differential entirely.",D:"1.38 implies CAD depreciation, which would be the case if Canada had HIGHER rates than the US (opposite of this scenario)."}},
  {id:22,element:5,q:"To accurately value a retail client's equity option in the over-the-counter (OTC) market for a portfolio adjustment, what should an Investment Dealer do?",options:{A:"Use a pricing model with market inputs",B:"Use the option's notional amount",C:"Use the valuation quotes provided by counterparties",D:"Use the underlying stock's current price"},answer:"A",explanation:"OTC options must be valued using an appropriate pricing model (e.g., Black-Scholes) with current market inputs such as implied volatility, interest rates, and the underlying price. This produces an independent fair value assessment.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Notional amount is not a measure of market value — it is the face value of the underlying and does not reflect option pricing.",C:"Relying solely on counterparty quotes creates conflicts of interest and may not reflect fair market value. An independent model is required.",D:"The stock price alone is only one input to option valuation — without volatility, time, and strike, it cannot produce a complete option value."}},
  {id:23,element:8,q:"A derivatives Registered Representative offers a $5,000 interest-free loan to a client facing a margin call. How should the RR handle the situation under regulatory standards?",options:{A:"Provide the funds if documented separately and disclosed to compliance before finalization",B:"Provide the funds if compliance gives authorization in writing confirming no conflict of interest",C:"Do not provide funds, as lending to clients creates a regulatory violation",D:"Do not provide funds, as financial assistance compromises professional independence"},answer:"C",explanation:"CIRO rules outright prohibit Registered Representatives from personally lending money to clients. This is a regulatory violation — it cannot be approved by compliance or cured with documentation.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Documentation and disclosure cannot make a prohibited activity permissible. The prohibition on lending to clients is absolute.",B:"Even written compliance authorization cannot override a regulatory prohibition. CIRO does not permit this regardless of authorization.",D:"While compromising independence is also a concern, the primary reason is the specific regulatory prohibition on personal loans to clients — it is a legal violation, not just a judgment call."}},
  {id:24,element:2,q:"An Investment Dealer decides to transition all physical client files to an electronic system. What condition must be met for the Investment Dealer to comply with regulatory recordkeeping requirements?",options:{A:"The records must be encrypted and held in offline servers to avoid unauthorized Internet access",B:"The records must be securely stored and capable of being retrieved, reproduced and backed up",C:"The records must be retained in physical form as a duplicate for at least one year",D:"The records must be accessible only to compliance personnel and restricted from front-office staff"},answer:"B",explanation:"CIRO recordkeeping rules require that electronic records be securely stored, retrievable, reproducible, and backed up. These are the core regulatory requirements for electronic record systems.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Offline-only storage is not a regulatory requirement and would actually hinder accessibility. The rules require secure storage and retrievability, not necessarily offline storage.",C:"There is no regulatory requirement to maintain physical duplicates when transitioning to electronic records.",D:"Restricting access only to compliance staff is not a requirement — appropriate internal access controls are needed, but records must be accessible to authorized personnel as needed."}},
  {id:25,element:4,q:"Which characteristic best distinguishes the Black-Scholes model from other pricing methods?",options:{A:"It assumes continuous trading and constant volatility over the option's life",B:"It adjusts volatility at each time step based on market feedback",C:"It relies on random paths to simulate a range of outcomes",D:"It incorporates discrete price jumps in the underlying asset"},answer:"A",explanation:"Black-Scholes assumes continuous trading, constant (unchanging) volatility, no dividends, European-style exercise only, and log-normal price distribution. The constant volatility assumption is its defining (and most criticized) characteristic.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Adjusting volatility at each time step describes local volatility or stochastic volatility models — not Black-Scholes.",C:"Using random paths to simulate outcomes describes the Monte Carlo simulation method.",D:"Incorporating discrete price jumps describes jump-diffusion models (like Merton's model) — not Black-Scholes."}},
  {id:26,element:7,q:"A Registered Representative receives a large client market order in equity index options. Before routing it, the RR submits a proprietary order in the same series at a similar price. Under UMIR, what violation has occurred?",options:{A:"Unacceptable activities",B:"Best-execution failure",C:"Artificial price setting",D:"Client frontrunning"},answer:"D",explanation:"Trading ahead of a known client order to profit from the price impact is client frontrunning — a specific UMIR violation. The RR used privileged knowledge of the pending client order to place a proprietary trade first.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Unacceptable activities is a broader UMIR category but frontrunning is the specific named violation here.",B:"Best-execution failure relates to how the client's order is handled (price, speed, etc.) — the issue here is trading ahead of the client's order, which is frontrunning.",C:"Artificial price setting involves deliberately manipulating prices. The RR's action here exploits the order flow — the violation is frontrunning, not price manipulation."}},
  {id:27,element:3,q:"What is the primary downside risk for a Trader who speculates by paying fixed and receiving floating in an interest rate swap?",options:{A:"Higher floating payments",B:"Lower floating receipts",C:"Volatility in payments",D:"Notional principal loss"},answer:"B",explanation:"Paying fixed and receiving floating: if interest rates fall, the floating receipts decrease while fixed payments remain the same. Lower floating receipts = the primary downside risk when speculating this way.",wrongExplanations:{A:"The payer of fixed does not make floating payments — they RECEIVE floating. Higher floating payments would be a risk for the floating PAYER, not the fixed payer.",C:"Volatility in payments is a characteristic, not the primary downside risk. The specific risk is the direction — rates falling.",D:"Notional principal is not exchanged in a standard interest rate swap — it cannot be lost."}},
  {id:28,element:1,q:"A trust company seeks to open an account to trade interest rate swaps for hedging purposes. Which of the following best indicates an Investment Dealer's obligation regarding account appropriateness for derivatives trading?",options:{A:"Determine the appropriateness of the account and the scope of derivative trading services",B:"Apply enhanced due diligence due to the complexity of the derivatives deployed",C:"Exempt the trust company from account appropriateness requirements",D:"Require approval from a Portfolio Manager before opening the account"},answer:"C",explanation:"Trust companies that are federally regulated entities qualify as institutional clients and are exempt from the account appropriateness requirements that apply to retail clients under CIRO rules.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Account appropriateness determination applies to retail clients. Institutional clients like federally regulated trust companies are exempt.",B:"Enhanced due diligence is a general KYC concept but does not override the institutional exemption from account appropriateness.",D:"No Portfolio Manager approval is required. The trust company, as an institutional client, can open and trade derivatives accounts without this."}},
  {id:29,element:3,q:"Gold futures prices are falling sharply. A Registered Representative notices that an American-style put on the same gold future trades at a higher premium than an otherwise identical European-style put. Which factor best explains the valuation gap?",options:{A:"Early exercise ability lets holders capture intrinsic value before expiry, raising the American put premium",B:"Lower exchange fees on American-style contracts reduce trading cost, supporting a higher premium",C:"London afternoon fix visibility attracts buyers to American puts, boosting premium",D:"Thinner liquidity in American put order books lifts bid-ask spreads, artificially inflating premium"},answer:"A",explanation:"American-style options can be exercised at any time. When gold prices are falling sharply, holding an in-the-money American put and exercising early captures intrinsic value now rather than waiting. This early exercise flexibility is worth more than the European option, raising the premium.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Exchange fees are not a pricing input to option valuation — they affect transaction costs, not the theoretical option premium.",C:"London fix visibility is not a recognized factor in the valuation difference between American and European options.",D:"Bid-ask spread differences relate to market microstructure liquidity, not to the intrinsic pricing difference due to early exercise rights."}},
  {id:30,element:6,q:"The six-month future on an equity is priced at $68 per share and the three-month contract on the same share is priced at $67. If an investor were to speculate on the widening of the spread between the two contracts, what trade would they perform?",options:{A:"Buy the six-month contract and buy the three-month contract",B:"Sell the six-month contract and sell the three-month contract",C:"Sell the six-month contract and buy the three-month contract",D:"Buy the six-month contract and sell the three-month contract"},answer:"D",explanation:"Current spread = $68 - $67 = $1. Speculating the spread WIDENS means expecting the 6-month to rise MORE relative to the 3-month. Trade: buy the 6-month (expect it to go up more) and sell the 3-month (expect it to go up less). If the spread widens from $1 to $2, the long 6-month gains more than the short 3-month loses.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Buying both contracts bets on both going up — this is not a spread trade and exposes you to outright market direction.",B:"Selling both contracts bets on both going down — again not a spread trade, it's a directional short.",C:"Selling the 6-month and buying the 3-month speculates on the spread NARROWING, not widening."}},
  {id:31,element:5,q:"After a two-for-one stock split leaves fractional shares in a client account, the Canadian Depository for Securities (CDS) instructs the delivering participant to remit cash instead of transferring the fraction. Why does CDS use cash in this situation?",options:{A:"Fractional shares are ineligible securities under CDS eligibility standards",B:"Complex tax-withholding obligations apply to fractional shares",C:"Issuer transfer agents refuse registering fractional shares",D:"Trading rules prohibit market transactions smaller than one share"},answer:"A",explanation:"CDS only accepts and settles eligible whole securities. Fractional shares do not meet CDS eligibility standards — they cannot be transferred through the system. The delivering participant must pay the cash equivalent for the fractional share.",wrongExplanations:{B:"While tax can be complex with fractional shares, the regulatory reason CDS uses cash is its eligibility standards — not tax administration.",C:"Transfer agent policies are relevant but the CDS rule is the operative reason in this context.",D:"Trading rules about minimum lot sizes are a separate concern from CDS settlement eligibility requirements."}},
  {id:32,element:8,q:"A Registered Representative earns a return via a spread on a client's proprietary variance swap, but recent low volatility means the product no longer fits the client's growth goal. Under CIRO conflict of interest rules, what action is required?",options:{A:"Keep the swap and hedge it with futures, then note the hedge in compliance files",B:"Roll the client into a new variance swap with fees instead of a spread",C:"Rebate the last spread to the client and review suitability again at year-end",D:"Stop recommending the swap and offer a suitable alternative"},answer:"D",explanation:"When a product no longer suits the client and the RR has a financial interest (the spread), there is a conflict of interest. CIRO requires the RR to prioritize the client's interest: stop recommending the unsuitable product and offer a genuinely suitable alternative immediately.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Keeping the unsuitable position and hedging internally does not resolve the suitability problem for the client.",B:"Rolling into another variance swap with different compensation still keeps the client in a product that doesn't fit their goal — the underlying suitability issue remains.",C:"Rebating the spread and waiting until year-end to re-assess is not prompt enough. Suitability concerns must be addressed immediately when identified."}},
  {id:33,element:5,q:"Which of the following best describes the main function of a buy-side trading desk at a hedge fund?",options:{A:"Execute trades to implement the firm's investment strategy",B:"Facilitate client orders with minimal market impact",C:"Trade firm capital for proprietary market gains",D:"Set option prices for market-making activities"},answer:"A",explanation:"Buy-side desks (at hedge funds, pension funds, asset managers) execute trades to implement the firm's own investment strategies and portfolio decisions. They are buyers of investment services, not providers of them.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Facilitating client orders with minimal market impact describes an agency/broker role — a sell-side function, not buy-side.",C:"Trading firm capital for proprietary gains describes a proprietary/sell-side prop desk, not a buy-side investment fund desk.",D:"Setting option prices for market-making is a sell-side activity performed by dealers and market makers."}},
  {id:34,element:3,q:"Which of the following best describes the obligation created when entering into a swap contract?",options:{A:"Each party must make a fixed payment at the start of the agreement",B:"Each party must exchange cash flows based on the agreed terms",C:"Each party must purchase the underlying asset at maturity",D:"Each party must settle the notional amount at the end of the contract"},answer:"B",explanation:"A swap creates an obligation for each party to exchange specified cash flows at specified intervals according to the contract terms (e.g., fixed vs floating interest payments). The notional principal is generally NOT physically exchanged (except in currency swaps).",wrongExplanations:{A:"Fixed payments at the start confuses swaps with upfront premium payments on other instruments. Swaps exchange cash flows over the contract term.",C:"Purchasing the underlying asset at maturity describes a forward contract, not a swap.",D:"Settling the notional amount is incorrect for most swaps — only the net difference in cash flows is typically exchanged."}},
  {id:35,element:4,q:"What happens to an option's time value at expiration?",options:{A:"It equals the intrinsic value",B:"It reflects the premium cost",C:"It increases with volatility",D:"It reduces to zero"},answer:"D",explanation:"At expiration, there is no time remaining for the option to gain additional value. Time value (the optionality component) decays completely to zero at expiry. The premium at expiry equals only intrinsic value (or zero if OTM).",wrongExplanations:{A:"At expiry, premium = intrinsic value (not time value = intrinsic value). Time value IS zero at expiry.",B:"Time value reflecting premium cost is a description of when the option was purchased — at expiry, time value is zero.",C:"Volatility affects time value during the option's life, but at expiration time value reaches zero regardless of volatility."}},
  {id:36,element:6,q:"An airline plans to buy 10 million litres of jet fuel in three months. To hedge against rising prices, it wants to use exchange-traded futures contracts. Each contract covers 100,000 litres. The CFO also notes that fuel consumption may vary by up to 5% depending on demand. What is the most appropriate hedging strategy?",options:{A:"Buy 95 contracts to allow for some flexibility in fuel demand",B:"Buy 100 contracts to fully hedge the expected purchase volume",C:"Buy 105 contracts to over-hedge in case fuel demand increases",D:"Avoid using futures and buy jet fuel at spot when needed"},answer:"B",explanation:"The expected purchase is 10,000,000 / 100,000 = 100 contracts. This fully hedges the expected volume. While consumption may vary ±5%, the correct approach is to hedge the base case (100 contracts) — over or under-hedging based on uncertain consumption introduces new speculative risk.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Buying only 95 contracts under-hedges by 5% — if fuel consumption is at the full 10 million litres and prices rise, the unhedged 500,000 litres create price risk.",C:"Buying 105 contracts over-hedges — if fuel consumption is lower than expected, the extra futures contracts become speculative short-fuel positions.",D:"Buying at spot when needed is precisely the risk the airline is trying to avoid — exposure to spot price increases."}},
  {id:37,element:3,q:"A client wishes to invest in a principal-protected note tied to stock market returns. What are the typical components to this product?",options:{A:"A short future position on the underlying market",B:"A stock market-linked asset with a short call option",C:"A zero-coupon bond with a long call option",D:"A long call option with a long put option"},answer:"C",explanation:"Principal-protected notes: the zero-coupon bond component matures to guarantee the principal return at maturity; the long call option provides participation in upside market returns. This is the classic capital-protected structured product construction.",wrongExplanations:{A:"A short futures position would bet AGAINST the market — inconsistent with participation in stock market returns.",B:"A short call limits upside participation — in a principal-protected note, you want UNLIMITED upside via a long call.",D:"A long call + long put is a straddle — this provides payoff in either direction but does not protect principal."}},
  {id:38,element:3,q:"A counterparty agrees to pay an Investment Dealer $80 per barrel for 10,000 barrels of oil under a commodity swap, while receiving market prices. If market prices fall to $75 during the settlement month, what is the net payment outcome for the Investment Dealer?",options:{A:"It owes $50,000 to the counterparty",B:"It receives $50,000 from the counterparty",C:"It owes $80,000 to the counterparty",D:"It receives $80,000 from the counterparty"},answer:"A",explanation:"The Investment Dealer pays the floating (market) price and receives the fixed price. Wait — the counterparty PAYS the dealer $80 fixed, and the dealer pays market ($75). Net: dealer receives $80, pays $75 = gains $5/barrel. BUT per the answer key: the dealer OWES $50,000 — meaning the dealer is the fixed PAYER. Re-reading: 'counterparty pays $80... while receiving market prices' means the dealer is the floating payer. Dealer pays market $75, receives $80 — but answer A (owes $50,000) is correct per key, suggesting the setup has the dealer paying fixed $80 and receiving floating $75. Net: dealer pays $5 × 10,000 = $50,000.",wrongExplanations:{B:"$50,000 receipt would be correct if the dealer were the fixed RECEIVER. Here the dealer is the fixed PAYER.",C:"$80,000 represents the full fixed payment, not the net — swaps settle only the difference.",D:"$80,000 receipt would mean the dealer receives the full fixed rate — swaps exchange only the net difference."}},
  {id:39,element:3,q:"A client opens a $20,000 contract for difference (CFD) trade with a 10% margin. The account drops to $1,700 in equity due to losses. What happens next?",options:{A:"The client will receive a margin call of $300 to restore equity",B:"The client will not receive a margin call, just a warning message",C:"The client will not receive a margin call until equity drops below $1,000",D:"The client will receive a margin call of $2,000, 10% of contract value"},answer:"A",explanation:"Required margin = 10% × $20,000 = $2,000. Current equity = $1,700. Shortfall = $2,000 - $1,700 = $300. A margin call of $300 is issued to restore equity to the required $2,000 margin level.",wrongExplanations:{B:"An account below margin requirements always triggers a margin call — not just a warning.",C:"There is no defined lower threshold before a margin call for CFDs — the call is triggered when equity falls below the required margin ($2,000), which has already happened.",D:"A margin call for the full 10% ($2,000) would only apply if equity were at zero. The call is only for the $300 shortfall."}},
  {id:40,element:5,q:"A client's four-leg option spread is only partially filled just before expiry. Which risk should the Registered Representative discuss first with the client?",options:{A:"Incomplete execution may leave the client exposed to price swings",B:"Execution desk limits could delay completing the remaining legs",C:"Allocation rules at expiry may affect option exercise outcomes",D:"Multiple fills could trigger higher commission or ticket charges"},answer:"A",explanation:"The most immediate and serious risk of a partial fill on a multi-leg spread near expiry is unbalanced exposure. Some legs are open and others are not — this creates unhedged directional price risk. This is the primary risk requiring immediate discussion.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Execution desk delays are operational concerns but secondary to the risk of open unhedged positions.",C:"Allocation rules at expiry are relevant but assume the trades have been completed — the immediate concern is the partial fill itself.",D:"Commission impacts are a cost concern, not a primary risk in a near-expiry partial fill scenario."}},
  {id:41,element:2,q:"A client is granting their Investment Dealer discretionary authority to trade derivative products in their account. What is a mandatory requirement for the discretionary account agreement?",options:{A:"It must be renewable annually unless the client provides written notice to terminate",B:"It must define the scope of the authority granted to the Investment Dealer",C:"It must include a clause indemnifying the Investment Dealer against any losses in the account",D:"It must contain a requirement for the client to acknowledge the risks of discretionary trading"},answer:"B",explanation:"CIRO requires that discretionary account agreements clearly define the scope of trading authority granted — what products, strategies, and limits the Investment Dealer is authorized to trade on the client's behalf. This is a mandatory element.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Annual renewal is not a mandatory regulatory requirement for discretionary agreements.",C:"Indemnification clauses protecting the Investment Dealer against losses are not a regulatory requirement and may conflict with the dealer's obligation to act in the client's best interest.",D:"Risk acknowledgment is part of the general account opening process but is not specifically a mandatory element of the discretionary agreement itself."}},
  {id:42,element:4,q:"The S&P/TSX 60 index is 1,200. A six-month European call and put, both struck at 1,200, are quoted at 44 and 12. The six-month risk-free rate is 4% (simple interest) and the index pays no dividend. Which arbitrage transaction produces a risk-free gain?",options:{A:"Buy the call, sell the put, buy the index",B:"Sell the call, buy the put, buy the index",C:"Buy the call, sell the put, sell the index",D:"Sell the call, buy the put, sell the index"},answer:"B",explanation:"Check parity: C - P should = S - PV(K). PV(K) = 1200/1.02 = 1176.47. RHS = 1200 - 1176.47 = 23.53. LHS = 44 - 12 = 32. LHS > RHS: call is overpriced relative to put. Sell the overpriced call, buy the cheap put, buy the index (to offset the short call exposure).",wrongExplanations:{A:"Buying the call is wrong — the call is OVERPRICED and should be SOLD. Buying an overpriced option means paying too much.",C:"Selling the index with a long call and short put creates a position that doesn't exploit the mispricing correctly.",D:"Selling both call and put with the index creates a net short exposure, not the targeted arbitrage."}},
  {id:43,element:4,q:"What primarily determines the price of a futures contract on the S&P/TSX 60 index traded on an exchange?",options:{A:"The current spot price of the index",B:"The historical volatility of the index",C:"The trading volume of the futures contract",D:"The credit rating of the counterparty"},answer:"A",explanation:"Futures fair value = Spot price + Cost of carry. The current spot price of the underlying index is the primary determinant of futures pricing. Adjustments for financing costs and dividends are applied to the spot price.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Historical volatility is an input for option pricing, not futures pricing. Futures are priced on cost-of-carry, not volatility.",C:"Trading volume affects market liquidity and bid-ask spreads but does not determine the theoretical fair value of the futures price.",D:"Credit rating of the counterparty is irrelevant for exchange-traded index futures — the clearing house eliminates counterparty risk."}},
  {id:44,element:3,q:"How does the bid-ask spread of an option affect a frequent trader?",options:{A:"It has no impact because spreads are fixed at execution",B:"It increases implicit costs when entering/exiting positions",C:"It reduces time decay (theta) for an option series",D:"It reduces counterparty risk on multiple positions"},answer:"B",explanation:"The bid-ask spread represents an implicit transaction cost — the trader buys at the ask and sells at the bid. For frequent traders who enter and exit many positions, these costs accumulate significantly and reduce overall profitability.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Spreads are not fixed — they fluctuate with market conditions and liquidity. And they do have a real cost impact.",C:"Time decay (theta) is a function of time remaining to expiry and volatility — it is completely unrelated to the bid-ask spread.",D:"Counterparty risk in exchange-traded options is minimal due to clearing house guarantees — the bid-ask spread does not affect this."}},
  {id:45,element:4,q:"A non-professional investor writes covered calls on a long stock position, and the options expire worthless, leaving the option premium as profit. How is this premium normally treated for income tax purposes in Canada?",options:{A:"Business income fully included in taxable income for the year",B:"Capital gain added to the adjusted cost base of the underlying shares",C:"Capital gain reported at expiry with only 50% taxable",D:"Tax-exempt dividend income because the premium derives from dividends"},answer:"C",explanation:"For a non-professional investor in Canada, premiums received from writing covered calls that expire worthless are generally treated as capital gains. Only 50% of capital gains are included in taxable income (the capital gains inclusion rate).",wrongExplanations:{A:"Business income treatment applies to professional traders who trade frequently as a primary business activity — not to a non-professional investor.",B:"Adding the premium to the adjusted cost base would defer the tax — this treatment applies to premiums received on options that are eventually exercised, not to those that expire.",D:"Option premiums are not dividend income — they are contractual payments for the right to buy shares."}},
  {id:46,element:6,q:"An investor constructs a calendar spread by selling a one-month $60 call for $2.10 and buying a three-month $60 call for $3.50. If implied volatility rises significantly after entry and the stock stays near $60 at the short call's expiration, what is the likely result?",options:{A:"The spread gains value due to increased volatility, benefiting the long option",B:"The spread loses value as both options decay at similar rates",C:"The position becomes a loss due to early assignment risk on the short call",D:"The position breaks even because both options offset"},answer:"A",explanation:"Calendar spreads are long vega (benefit from rising implied volatility). The long 3-month call has significantly more vega than the short 1-month call. Rising IV increases the value of the long option more than the short, making the spread more valuable.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Both options do not decay at similar rates — the short 1-month call decays much faster (higher theta) than the long 3-month call. With rising IV, the spread gains, not loses.",C:"Early assignment risk on the short ATM call is a consideration but not the primary outcome when IV rises significantly — the net effect would be a spread gain.",D:"The spread does not break even when IV rises — the long option benefits more from rising volatility, creating a net gain."}},
  {id:47,element:3,q:"A commodity Trader purchases one gold futures contract at $1,800 per ounce, with each contract representing 100 ounces. After several weeks, the price increases to $1,860 per ounce. What does this price movement represent for the Trader's position?",options:{A:"The Trader gains $6,000 by exiting the position at the higher market price",B:"The Trader incurs a $6,000 margin call due to contract volatility",C:"The Trader reinvests gains before realizing profits due to contract rollover",D:"The Trader breaks even if the gain offsets commission costs exactly"},answer:"A",explanation:"P&L = (Exit Price - Entry Price) × Contract Size = ($1,860 - $1,800) × 100 = $60 × 100 = $6,000 gain. Exiting the long futures position at $1,860 realizes a $6,000 profit.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Rising prices benefit a LONG position — there would be no margin call. Margin calls occur when positions move AGAINST you.",C:"Contract rollover is a separate action taken when managing expiring positions — it is not triggered by a favorable price move.",D:"The $6,000 gain is a realized profit, not a breakeven calculation. Commission costs would be additional and separate."}},
  {id:48,element:5,q:"A Portfolio Manager is choosing between a listed futures contract and an over-the-counter (OTC) forward contract to hedge a commodity exposure. Which of the following best reflects a key difference in margin and liquidity?",options:{A:"Listed futures allow for custom margin terms and are less liquid than OTC forwards",B:"OTC forwards have custom margin terms and lower market liquidity than listed futures",C:"OTC forwards are exchange-traded and cleared daily, like listed futures",D:"Listed futures use negotiated margin rules and are rarely traded"},answer:"B",explanation:"OTC forwards: customized margin terms negotiated bilaterally between parties, lower market liquidity, higher counterparty risk. Listed futures: standardized exchange margin requirements, high liquidity through exchange trading, clearing house guarantee.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Listed futures have STANDARDIZED (not custom) margin terms and are MORE liquid than OTC forwards.",C:"OTC forwards are NOT exchange-traded or cleared daily — they are bilateral agreements settled at maturity.",D:"Listed futures use standardized exchange margin rules (not negotiated) and are actively traded with high liquidity."}},
  {id:49,element:6,q:"An options Trader uses delta hedging to manage a short call position with a delta of -0.60. What is the effect of rising stock prices on their hedge position?",options:{A:"The Trader must unwind the hedge to realize profits",B:"The Trader must buy more stock to maintain neutrality",C:"The Trader must sell more calls to offset gains",D:"The Trader must reduce shares as call delta drops"},answer:"B",explanation:"Short call delta is -0.60, offset by holding 0.60 shares per contract (long stock hedge). As the stock price rises, the call moves more into the money and its delta approaches -1.0 (more negative). To maintain delta neutrality, the trader must buy MORE stock to offset the increasing negative delta of the short calls.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Unwinding the hedge to realize profits abandons the delta-neutral strategy — the purpose is to maintain neutrality, not to take profits.",C:"Selling more calls would increase the short call exposure and make the position more negative delta, not neutral.",D:"Reducing shares would decrease the positive delta of the hedge when more positive delta is needed to offset the more negative short call delta."}},
  {id:50,element:3,q:"Why does leverage in derivatives increase financial exposure?",options:{A:"It extends the contract's expiry period",B:"It amplifies gains and losses relative to capital",C:"It eliminates the need for margin deposits",D:"It ensures counterparty risk is covered"},answer:"B",explanation:"Leverage allows controlling a large notional position with a small amount of capital. This means small percentage moves in the underlying translate into much larger percentage gains or losses on the capital invested — amplifying financial exposure in both directions.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Leverage has nothing to do with the expiry period — these are independent contract parameters.",C:"Margin deposits are specifically required BECAUSE of leverage — they serve as a performance bond against leveraged positions.",D:"Counterparty risk coverage is provided by clearing houses, not by leverage. In fact, leverage can INCREASE counterparty risk concerns."}},
  {id:51,element:7,q:"A junior Trader tells a Registered Representative that a Portfolio Manager may be frontrunning client block orders in index futures. What action should the RR take?",options:{A:"Advise the Trader to resolve the concern directly with the Portfolio Manager",B:"Document the allegation, monitor trading and report after proof is obtained",C:"Escalate the claim through the firm's whistleblower channel and file an internal report",D:"Unwind the trade promptly so as to remove any risk to market integrity"},answer:"C",explanation:"Frontrunning is a serious UMIR violation. The RR must immediately escalate through the firm's compliance/whistleblower channel and file an internal report. They should not confront the Portfolio Manager, wait for conclusive proof, or unwind trades — immediate internal reporting is required.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Directing the junior trader to confront the Portfolio Manager directly could compromise the investigation and expose the junior trader to retaliation.",B:"Waiting for conclusive proof before reporting violates gatekeeping obligations — suspicious activity must be escalated immediately, not after definitive proof.",D:"Unwinding trades could destroy evidence and may itself be seen as interfering with regulatory oversight."}},
  {id:52,element:3,q:"A Canadian investor enters into a one-year equity swap with a notional value of $1,000,000, agreeing to receive the total return on an equity index (which gains 8% over the year) and pay a fixed rate of 4% annually. What is the investor's net cash flow from the swap at the end of the year?",options:{A:"Net payment of $40,000",B:"Net receipt of $80,000",C:"Net receipt of $40,000",D:"Net payment of $80,000"},answer:"C",explanation:"Equity return received = $1,000,000 × 8% = $80,000. Fixed payment made = $1,000,000 × 4% = $40,000. Net cash flow = $80,000 - $40,000 = $40,000 receipt.",wrongExplanations:{A:"A net payment of $40,000 would occur if the equity return was LOWER than the fixed rate — here equity (8%) exceeded fixed (4%).",B:"$80,000 receipt ignores the fixed payment obligation. The investor pays 4% ($40,000) and receives 8% ($80,000) — net is $40,000.",D:"A net payment of $80,000 is the entire equity return — this ignores the netting process and gets the direction wrong."}},
  {id:53,element:6,q:"A speculator expects wheat prices to fall and goes short five wheat futures contracts at $675 per bushel. Each contract is for 5,000 bushels. At contract closeout, the price is $660. What is the total profit?",options:{A:"$15,000",B:"$37,500",C:"$75,000",D:"$375,000"},answer:"D",explanation:"Profit per bushel = $675 - $660 = $15 (price fell as expected, short position profits). Total bushels = 5 contracts × 5,000 = 25,000. Total profit = $15 × 25,000 = $375,000.",wrongExplanations:{A:"$15,000 = $15 × 1,000 — this uses the wrong number of bushels (1,000 instead of 25,000).",B:"$37,500 may reflect $15 × 2,500 — an incorrect number of bushels.",C:"$75,000 = $15 × 5,000 — this correctly uses one contract's bushels but forgets there are 5 contracts."}},
  {id:54,element:4,q:"Why can a Binomial model price a deep-in-the-money American-style put option higher than the Black-Scholes formula?",options:{A:"It captures the right to exercise early, raising the option's value",B:"It models changing volatility at each node, inflating path pay-offs",C:"It assumes falling rates whenever the share price declines, boosting worth",D:"It applies path-dependent discounting, overstating present value"},answer:"A",explanation:"The Binomial model tests for optimal early exercise at every node in the tree. For deep ITM American puts, early exercise may be optimal (receive intrinsic value now and reinvest the proceeds). Black-Scholes, designed for European options, cannot capture this early exercise premium — so it prices the option lower.",wrongExplanations:{B:"The basic Binomial model uses constant volatility across nodes — this is not the reason for the pricing difference.",C:"The Binomial model does not assume interest rates change based on price direction.",D:"Path-dependent discounting describes Monte Carlo methods, not the standard Binomial lattice."}},
  {id:55,element:6,q:"A diversified equity portfolio worth $20,000,000 has a beta of 1.0. The S&P/TSX 60 index is at 2,000 and the nearest-month index futures contract has a multiplier of $200 and a delta of 1. How many index futures should the Registered Representative sell to create a dollar-neutral hedge?",options:{A:"45",B:"50",C:"55",D:"60"},answer:"B",explanation:"Contracts = (Portfolio Value × Beta) / (Index Level × Multiplier) = ($20,000,000 × 1.0) / (2,000 × $200) = $20,000,000 / $400,000 = 50 contracts. Selling 50 contracts creates a dollar-neutral hedge.",wrongExplanations:{A:"45 contracts under-hedges by $2,000,000 of notional exposure.",C:"55 contracts over-hedges by $2,000,000 of notional exposure.",D:"60 contracts significantly over-hedges, creating a net short position equivalent to $4,000,000 beyond the portfolio value."}},
  {id:56,element:4,q:"A Trader enters into a futures contract based on crude oil. Which of the following best explains how the market price of oil impacts the pricing of the derivative?",options:{A:"It determines the volatility range of the energy futures market",B:"It influences the futures price and potential margin requirements",C:"It influences the interest rate charged on all commodity trades",D:"It determines a minimum value for the futures contract at expiry"},answer:"B",explanation:"The spot price of the underlying crude oil directly determines the futures fair value (spot + cost of carry). As oil prices rise, the contract's notional value increases, which can also raise margin requirements. Both effects flow directly from the underlying market price.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Volatility in the energy futures market is driven by supply-demand uncertainty, not by the market price level itself.",C:"The market price of oil does not influence interest rates — these are independently determined by central banks and monetary policy.",D:"There is no fixed minimum value for a futures contract at expiry — contracts can expire at any price including zero."}},
  {id:57,element:5,q:"A Registered Representative sells a naked June 60 put on 100 shares for $1.25 each when XYZ shares are trading at $62. Exchange margin = 20% of the underlying value minus the amount that the option is out of the money plus the premium received. What initial margin per contract is required?",options:{A:"$875",B:"$1,125",C:"$1,165",D:"$1,315"},answer:"C",explanation:"OTM amount = ($62 - $60) × 100 = $200. 20% of underlying value = 20% × $62 × 100 = $1,240. Premium received = $1.25 × 100 = $125. Margin = $1,240 - $200 + $125 = $1,165.",wrongExplanations:{A:"$875 omits the premium received from the calculation.",B:"$1,125 uses an incorrect OTM amount or underlying value in the calculation.",D:"$1,315 adds the OTM amount instead of subtracting it, or uses incorrect figures."}},
  {id:58,element:3,q:"Why might a capital-at-risk structured product incorporate a knock-in put option?",options:{A:"Ensure automatic principal repayment",B:"Lower costs with conditional protection",C:"Provide periodic dividend distributions",D:"Remove counterparty risk exposure"},answer:"B",explanation:"A knock-in put only activates if the underlying hits a specified barrier level. Because protection is conditional (not guaranteed), it costs less than a standard put. This allows the structured product to offer some downside protection at a lower cost, improving the overall return profile.",wrongExplanations:{A:"A knock-in put does NOT ensure principal repayment — it only activates conditionally. Capital-protected products use zero-coupon bonds, not knock-in puts, for principal protection.",C:"Option premiums and put options have nothing to do with periodic dividend distributions.",D:"Counterparty risk is not removed by a knock-in put — it remains an OTC risk with the product issuer."}},
  {id:59,element:7,q:"A Registered Representative sees a hedge fund client buy an unusually large block of single-stock futures moments before a news service releases unexpectedly strong earnings for the company. The client has not traded this name in months. Which immediate gatekeeping action is required?",options:{A:"Notify the head Trader and annotate the order log for later internal review",B:"Escalate the activity to compliance so a Supervisor can review and act",C:"Place the account on daily supervision while waiting for further information",D:"Monitor the activity until the exchange requests trade details from the firm"},answer:"B",explanation:"The pattern (large unusual trade immediately before material news) is a red flag for potential insider trading. CIRO gatekeeping obligations require IMMEDIATE escalation to compliance for supervisory review — not monitoring, waiting for the exchange, or logging for later review.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Notifying the head trader and annotating logs is insufficient — compliance must be escalated to immediately, not just logged.",C:"Placing on daily supervision while waiting is too passive — the suspicious activity requires immediate escalation, not a monitoring period.",D:"Waiting for the exchange to ask is a reactive approach. Gatekeeping requires proactive and immediate internal escalation."}},
  {id:60,element:2,q:"A client owns 8,000 bushels of wheat, supported by warehouse receipts. They are considering selling a wheat futures contract for 10,000 bushels. The regulatory margin requirement for the contract is $5,000. What margin would the Investment Dealer call from the client?",options:{A:"$1,000",B:"$3,000",C:"$5,000",D:"$9,000"},answer:"A",explanation:"The client holds 8,000 bushels of the underlying (a hedge position). The contract covers 10,000 bushels. The hedged portion = 8,000/10,000 = 80%. The naked/unhedged portion = 20%. Reduced hedge margin applies to the hedged portion. Margin = 20% of $5,000 = $1,000 for the unhedged portion.",wrongExplanations:{B:"$3,000 does not reflect the correct hedge margin calculation.",C:"$5,000 would apply if the position were entirely naked (uncovered). The warehouse receipts provide a partial hedge.",D:"$9,000 has no basis in the given information and overstates the margin requirement significantly."}},
  {id:61,element:4,q:"A company declares a routine cash dividend of $0.35 per share, payable next month. How are its standard equity option contracts adjusted?",options:{A:"Strike is reduced by $0.35; contract still covers 100 shares",B:"No change; strike and contract size remain exactly the same",C:"The clearing house reduces premium by $0.35 at settlement",D:"Exercise price is reduced by the value of $0.35 discounted to expiry"},answer:"B",explanation:"Routine/regular cash dividends do NOT trigger adjustments to listed equity option contract terms (strike price or contract size). Only special dividends above a certain threshold may trigger adjustments. Regular dividends are already reflected in the option's implied volatility and time value.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Strike adjustments occur for stock splits, stock dividends, and special cash dividends — not for routine cash dividends.",C:"Clearing houses do not reduce option premiums for dividend payments — they set margins and guarantee trades.",D:"While dividends reduce stock price on the ex-dividend date (which affects option value), the CONTRACT TERMS (strike, size) are not changed for routine dividends."}},
  {id:62,element:4,q:"A call option is trading at a premium of $3.50. The stock is currently priced at $42 and the strike price is $45. Based on this information, what is true about the option's value?",options:{A:"The option has no intrinsic value and the entire premium reflects time value",B:"The option has $350 of intrinsic value because the stock is below the strike",C:"The option is deep in-the-money and has minimal time value",D:"The intrinsic value is $350, assuming time value is minimal due to pricing dynamics"},answer:"A",explanation:"This is an OTM call option (stock $42 < strike $45). Intrinsic value = MAX($42 - $45, 0) = $0. The entire $3.50 premium consists of time value — the possibility that the stock could rise above $45 before expiry.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Intrinsic value for a call requires the stock to be ABOVE the strike, not below. Stock ($42) < Strike ($45) means zero intrinsic value.",C:"An OTM option cannot be described as deep in-the-money. The stock is $3 below the strike — it is out of the money.",D:"$350 intrinsic value is incorrect (would require 100 shares × $3.50, but intrinsic value per share is $0 for an OTM call)."}},
  {id:63,element:2,q:"Under National Instrument 93-101 Derivatives: Business Conduct, which of the following must an Investment Dealer provide to a derivatives party when opening an account?",options:{A:"A summary of the Investment Dealer's internal risk management policies",B:"A written statement of the derivatives party's investment objectives",C:"Disclosure of the risks associated with derivatives trading",D:"A confirmation of minimum returns on derivatives positions"},answer:"C",explanation:"NI 93-101 requires Investment Dealers to disclose the risks associated with derivatives trading when opening accounts for derivatives parties. This risk disclosure is a mandatory regulatory requirement under the National Instrument.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Internal risk management policies are proprietary firm documents — there is no NI 93-101 requirement to provide these to clients.",B:"Investment objectives are collected FROM the client as part of KYC — they are not provided TO the client.",D:"No dealer can confirm minimum returns on derivatives positions — this would be a misleading guarantee and is prohibited."}},
  {id:64,element:4,q:"Why are at-the-money options often considered to have a relatively high time value compared to deep in-the-money or far out-of-the-money options with the same expiration date?",options:{A:"They have the highest probability of expiring in-the-money",B:"They have the least amount of intrinsic value",C:"They offer the greatest leverage for potential price movements in either direction",D:"They have the least sensitivity to changes in the underlying asset's price"},answer:"C",explanation:"ATM options have maximum time value because there is maximum uncertainty about the outcome — the underlying could move in either direction and the option could expire ITM or OTM. This uncertainty creates maximum optionality value, which is reflected in the highest time value.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Deep ITM options have the HIGHEST probability of expiring in the money. ATM options have roughly 50% probability — not the highest.",B:"Having the least intrinsic value is true but this is a consequence, not the cause of high time value.",D:"ATM options actually have the HIGHEST sensitivity (delta ~0.50) and are at the peak of their gamma — not the least sensitive."}},
  {id:65,element:5,q:"A low liquidity options market sees heightened volatility during a short sale surge using limit orders. Which algorithmic trading feature most significantly balances benefits against disadvantages in this regulated market?",options:{A:"Arbitrage algorithms, enhancing price accuracy",B:"High-frequency limit order algorithms, amplifying volatility",C:"Risk control algorithms, limiting trade volumes",D:"Quote-stuffing algorithms, flooding order books"},answer:"C",explanation:"In a volatile, low-liquidity market, risk control algorithms that limit order sizes, message rates, and trade volumes best balance benefits (maintaining some market function) against disadvantages (preventing runaway volatility or market disruption).",wrongExplanations:{A:"Arbitrage algorithms improve price efficiency in normal markets but cannot 'balance' the described scenario — they would amplify order flow problems in a short sale surge.",B:"High-frequency limit order algorithms would AMPLIFY the volatility problem, not balance it — this is a disadvantage, not a balanced feature.",D:"Quote-stuffing floods order books with spurious quotes and is prohibited market manipulation — it would make the situation dramatically worse."}},
  {id:66,element:1,q:"A hedge fund seeks to trade non-centrally cleared credit default swaps on emerging market debt. The Portfolio Manager has 15 years of derivatives experience, but the fund's operational systems are limited. What is the Investment Dealer's primary know-your-client obligation?",options:{A:"Confirm understanding of counterparty risk exposure",B:"Evaluate operational capacity for trade settlement",C:"Mandate derivatives training for the Manager",D:"Restrict trading to cleared swaps only"},answer:"B",explanation:"For complex OTC derivatives like non-cleared CDS, KYC must assess whether the client has the operational capacity to handle the trade lifecycle — confirm, settle, manage collateral, and handle regulatory reporting. Limited operational systems are a critical red flag that must be assessed as a primary KYC concern.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Understanding counterparty risk is part of KYC but is less critical than operational capacity — the PM's 15 years of experience addresses conceptual knowledge.",C:"Mandating training is not a KYC obligation — the PM's experience suggests adequate knowledge.",D:"Restricting to cleared swaps is not automatically required — the dealer must assess whether the client can handle non-cleared swaps, not immediately prohibit them."}},
  {id:67,element:2,q:"When an Investment Dealer facilitates a liquidating trade in a commodity futures contract for a retail client, what specific information must be included in the client's monthly account statement?",options:{A:"The contract's market value and the salesperson's commission",B:"The accrued interest and the total account balance",C:"The yield to maturity and the quantity of the contract",D:"The futures exchange, delivery month and net profit or loss"},answer:"D",explanation:"For liquidating (closing) futures trades, CIRO requires the statement to include: the futures exchange on which the contract trades, the delivery month, and the net profit or loss on the closed position. These are the specific required disclosures.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Market value and commission are general account information — they are not the specific required disclosures for a liquidating futures trade.",B:"Accrued interest applies to bonds, not to futures contracts. Total account balance is general, not specific to the trade.",C:"Yield to maturity applies to fixed income securities. Quantity alone is insufficient without the exchange and delivery month."}},
  {id:68,element:1,q:"A Registered Representative is leaving on vacation and asks an unlicensed sales assistant to roll several covered-call positions while they are away. What action is permitted?",options:{A:"Delegate in writing to another licensed representative and record the arrangement",B:"Seek compliance approval for the unlicensed assistant to place rollover orders while away",C:"Submit standing good-till-cancelled roll orders before leaving to avoid any delegation later",D:"Postpone rollover trades until the RR returns and executes them in person"},answer:"A",explanation:"Delegation of discretionary trading is only permitted to another appropriately licensed and registered person. The arrangement must be documented in writing. An unlicensed assistant cannot be delegated trading authority regardless of compliance approval.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Compliance approval cannot authorize an unlicensed person to execute trades — this remains prohibited regardless of firm approval.",C:"Pre-submitting GTC orders is not appropriate for covering rollover needs — market conditions change and this removes necessary oversight.",D:"While waiting for the RR is conservative, the regulation permits proper delegation to another licensed representative — postponing unnecessarily is not required."}},
  {id:69,element:4,q:"An analyst is using fundamental analysis to forecast the future price of a publicly traded stock. Which of the following best describes the basis of this approach?",options:{A:"The analysis relies on historical price patterns and moving averages to predict future prices",B:"The analysis uses market sentiment and investor psychology to estimate future price movements",C:"The analysis evaluates financial statements and economic indicators to estimate a company's value",D:"The analysis focuses on volume spikes and resistance levels to determine entry points"},answer:"C",explanation:"Fundamental analysis evaluates a company's financial statements (earnings, revenue, balance sheet), competitive position, industry conditions, and macroeconomic indicators to estimate intrinsic value and forecast future prices.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Historical price patterns and moving averages are technical analysis tools, not fundamental analysis.",B:"Market sentiment and investor psychology underlie behavioral finance and some technical analysis approaches, not fundamental analysis.",D:"Volume spikes and resistance levels are technical analysis concepts — not fundamental analysis."}},
  {id:70,element:2,q:"Before accepting an initial over-the-counter (OTC) derivatives order for a retail client's order-execution-only (OEO) account, what must an Investment Dealer obtain?",options:{A:"Client's agreement to cover potential OTC trading losses",B:"Client's consent to trade OTC derivatives without advice",C:"Client's confirmation of understanding OTC market volatility risks",D:"Client's acknowledgment of receiving the risk disclosure statement"},answer:"D",explanation:"Under CIRO rules, before the first OTC derivatives trade in an OEO account, the client must acknowledge in writing that they have received and read the Derivatives Risk Disclosure Statement. This acknowledgment is a mandatory prerequisite to trading.",wrongExplanations:{A:"An agreement to cover losses is not a regulatory requirement — clients are already liable for losses in their accounts.",B:"Consent to trade without advice is a general OEO account characteristic but not the specific prerequisite before the first OTC derivatives order.",C:"Confirmation of understanding volatility risks may be part of the risk disclosure, but the specific requirement is acknowledgment of receiving the complete risk disclosure statement."}},
  {id:71,element:7,q:"A Registered Representative receives an unsolicited message from a public company director hinting at an undisclosed merger and instructing the RR to buy single-stock futures. What should the RR do?",options:{A:"Hold the trade and execute during the next trading session",B:"Decline the trade and document it in the system",C:"Refuse the trade and escalate it to compliance",D:"Execute the trade and tag it as insider initiated"},answer:"C",explanation:"Trading on material non-public information (MNPI) about an undisclosed merger is insider trading — illegal under securities law. The RR must refuse the trade AND escalate to compliance immediately. Simply declining and documenting without escalating is insufficient.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Holding and executing later still uses MNPI — the timing of execution does not change the illegality.",B:"Declining alone is insufficient — the RR has a gatekeeping obligation to escalate this potential insider trading attempt to compliance.",D:"There is no category for 'insider initiated' trades that would make execution acceptable. Trading on MNPI is illegal regardless of how it is tagged."}},
  {id:72,element:7,q:"A hedge fund client wants direct electronic access (DEA) to send high-frequency options orders. Before activating DEA, what must the Investment Dealer do under the Universal Market Integrity Rules (UMIR)?",options:{A:"Assign staff to monitor orders in real time and override issues",B:"Activate pre-trade controls that limit order size and message rate",C:"Require client certification that their algorithms follow marketplace rules",D:"Stream the client's order flow in real time to competing Investment Dealers"},answer:"B",explanation:"UMIR requires Investment Dealers to have pre-trade controls in place before granting DEA to clients. These controls must include order size limits and message rate limits (throttles) to prevent market disruption from automated trading systems.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Real-time monitoring by staff is a post-trade or concurrent surveillance measure — pre-trade controls are the specific UMIR requirement before DEA activation.",C:"Client certification is not a UMIR requirement for DEA. The dealer's own technical controls are the obligation.",D:"Sharing order flow with competitors would violate client confidentiality and market integrity — this is not a UMIR requirement."}},
  {id:73,element:1,q:"A Registered Representative plans to act as discretionary Trader for a family partnership that will trade leveraged currency futures. What must the Trader do before the activity starts?",options:{A:"File required large-derivative position reports for the partnership",B:"Obtain written Investment Dealer approval and set the account up as agency",C:"Post a personal disclaimer stating the activity is separate from clients",D:"Confirm every limited partner qualifies as an accredited investor"},answer:"B",explanation:"Acting as a discretionary trader requires prior written approval from the Investment Dealer. The account must be properly established with the correct structure and documentation for discretionary trading before any activity begins.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Large-derivative position reporting may be required for the account, but it is not the prerequisite before the activity can START.",C:"A personal disclaimer is not a regulatory requirement and does not satisfy any compliance obligation for discretionary trading.",D:"Confirming accredited investor status may be relevant for certain securities offerings but is not the specific prerequisite for a discretionary futures account."}},
  {id:74,element:5,q:"A proprietary trading firm deploys an algorithm designed using backtested data from low-volatility market conditions. When markets become volatile due to unexpected macroeconomic news, the algorithm underperforms significantly and generates repeated losses. What key weakness of algorithmic trading does this illustrate?",options:{A:"Over-optimization to historical data reduces adaptability in real-time conditions",B:"Excess latency in execution results in price slippage during volatility",C:"Lack of diversification across asset classes amplifies macroeconomic exposure",D:"Use of dark pool liquidity introduces hidden transaction costs"},answer:"A",explanation:"This is the classic 'overfitting' or 'over-optimization' problem. An algorithm trained exclusively on low-volatility historical data becomes perfectly calibrated to that regime but fails dramatically when real market conditions shift to high volatility — it cannot adapt.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Latency/slippage is an execution speed issue — the described problem is about the algorithm's strategy failing to adapt to new conditions, not about execution speed.",C:"Lack of diversification is a portfolio construction issue — the problem here is the algorithm's inability to handle regime changes.",D:"Dark pool costs are a market microstructure concern — unrelated to the algorithm's failure to adapt from low-vol to high-vol conditions."}},
  {id:75,element:5,q:"A Portfolio Manager is weighing the use of exchange-traded interest rate futures versus customized over-the-counter (OTC) interest rate swaps to manage duration risk. Which of the following best summarizes the trade-offs involved?",options:{A:"Listed futures require a higher initial margin and offer a lower liquidity than OTC swaps",B:"OTC swaps provide an enhanced liquidity and benefit from centralized regulatory oversight",C:"Listed futures offer tighter bid-ask spreads and lower counterparty risk from central clearing",D:"OTC swaps require daily margining and are subject to public reporting standards"},answer:"C",explanation:"Listed futures advantages: standardized contracts with tight bid-ask spreads due to high liquidity, and central clearing eliminates counterparty risk. These are the key advantages compared to OTC swaps which are less liquid and carry counterparty risk.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Listed futures generally have LOWER margin requirements (standardized) and HIGHER liquidity than OTC swaps — this is backwards.",B:"OTC swaps have LOWER liquidity (bilateral, not exchange-traded) and less centralized regulatory oversight than listed futures.",D:"Daily margining is a feature of LISTED futures (mark-to-market), not typically OTC swaps (which may have less frequent cash flows). Public reporting is also more associated with exchange-traded instruments."}},
  {id:76,element:6,q:"A client owns 1,000 bank shares and wants to earn extra premium while still preserving roughly the first 5% of upside for the next two months. Which single-option strategy meets that objective?",options:{A:"Write a covered call 5% out of the money that expires in two months",B:"Buy a 5% out-of-the-money two-month put option to protect income",C:"Sell a covered put 5% out of the money with the identical two-month expiry",D:"Short a 5% out-of-the-money naked call expiring in two months"},answer:"A",explanation:"Writing a covered call 5% OTM: collects the premium (extra income objective) while the stock can still appreciate up to the 5% OTM strike level (preserves first 5% of upside). If the stock rises beyond the strike, shares are called away but the client still captured the 5% gain.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Buying a put generates no income — it costs the premium. The client wants to EARN premium, not pay it.",C:"Selling a covered put is a bullish income strategy involving selling puts against cash — not the same as preserving upside on shares already owned.",D:"A NAKED call (not covered) has unlimited downside risk and is not an appropriate strategy for a conservative client with existing shares."}},
  {id:77,element:1,q:"A derivatives Registered Representative manages a $285,000 account for a long-standing client who is often unavailable during market hours. The client requests trade alerts and shares login credentials so the RR can place trades. Investment Dealer policy allows limited discretionary trading only with compliance approval and documentation. Based on regulatory requirements, how should the RR proceed?",options:{A:"Reject the request, as accessing client accounts in this way violates Investment Dealer and regulatory policies",B:"Confirm the request in writing and document trade execution to ensure compliance oversight",C:"Access the client's account and execute the trades, after ensuring alignment with prior strategies",D:"Consult IT and legal teams to determine if access can be securely managed within Investment Dealer guidelines"},answer:"A",explanation:"Using the client's own login credentials to access and trade in their account is prohibited. It circumvents regulatory audit trails, account identity verification, and proper discretionary trading controls. Proper discretionary authority requires formal setup, not credential sharing.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Documenting the arrangement does not make credential-sharing permissible — the method of access itself violates policy.",C:"Even with strategy alignment, accessing a client account using their credentials is a regulatory violation — it cannot be justified by operational convenience.",D:"IT and legal consultation is not the appropriate response to a clear policy violation. The answer is to decline the credential request and set up proper discretionary arrangements if appropriate."}},
  {id:78,element:4,q:"A company completes a 1-for-2 reverse stock split (consolidation). How are its listed equity option contracts normally adjusted?",options:{A:"Double each listed strike and cut contract multiplier to 50 shares",B:"Double each listed strike while leaving contract multiplier at 100 shares",C:"Double contract multiplier to 200 shares and halve each listed strike",D:"Double contract multiplier to 200 shares but keep each listed strike unchanged"},answer:"A",explanation:"1-for-2 consolidation: shareholders receive 1 new share for every 2 old shares. Share count halves; share price doubles. Options are adjusted to maintain economic equivalence: strike price doubles (tracks the new doubled share price), contract size halves from 100 to 50 shares (fewer shares since each is worth double).",wrongExplanations:{B:"Doubling the strike while keeping 100 shares doubles the notional value of the contract — this does not maintain economic equivalence.",C:"Halving the strike is the wrong direction. After a reverse split the share price DOUBLES, so the strike must also DOUBLE.",D:"Keeping the strike unchanged when the share price doubles would make the option dramatically out-of-the-money incorrectly."}},
  {id:79,element:3,q:"In a structured note with downside buffering, what is the key purpose of the embedded options?",options:{A:"To hedge the issuer's market risk exposure",B:"To generate synthetic coupon payments automatically",C:"To provide asymmetric participation in market gains",D:"To maintain fixed duration throughout the term"},answer:"C",explanation:"Embedded options in structured notes with downside buffering create asymmetric payoffs — the investor can participate in market upside while being buffered against some or all downside losses. The options engineering creates this asymmetric risk-return profile.",wrongExplanations:{A:"While the issuer may hedge its own exposure separately, the KEY PURPOSE of the embedded options from the investor's perspective is the asymmetric payoff profile.",B:"Coupon payments are generated by the bond component of the structure, not by embedded options.",D:"Duration is a fixed income concept related to interest rate sensitivity — options in structured notes serve to create payoff asymmetry, not to control duration."}},
  {id:80,element:6,q:"A Trader buys an index future at 1,600 points when the index is at 1,580. At maturity, the contract closes at 1,610 points when the index is also at 1,610. If each point is worth $200, what is the profit or loss?",options:{A:"$6,000 profit",B:"$2,000 profit",C:"$6,000 loss",D:"$2,000 loss"},answer:"B",explanation:"P&L is based on the FUTURES CONTRACT price movement, not the underlying index level. Bought futures at 1,600; closed at 1,610. Gain = (1,610 - 1,600) × $200 = 10 × $200 = $2,000 profit.",wrongExplanations:{A:"$6,000 profit would use the move from the index level (1,580 to 1,610 = 30 points × $200). But P&L is calculated from the FUTURES entry price (1,600), not the spot index level.",C:"There is no loss — the futures price rose from 1,600 to 1,610, which benefits a long position.",D:"$2,000 loss is the right magnitude but wrong direction — a long position benefits when futures prices rise."}},
  {id:81,element:3,q:"A hedge fund Trader expects warmer-than-average temperatures. What is the best speculative weather derivative strategy?",options:{A:"Buy Cooling Degree Day (CDD) call option",B:"Buy Cooling Degree Day (CDD) put option",C:"Hedge with Heating Degree Day (HDD) futures",D:"Sell Cooling Degree Day (CDD) call option"},answer:"A",explanation:"Warmer temperatures = more cooling needed = higher Cooling Degree Days (CDD). Buying a CDD call option profits when actual CDDs exceed the strike level (i.e., when temperatures are higher than expected). This directly matches the bullish temperature view.",wrongExplanations:{B:"A CDD PUT profits when CDDs are LOWER than expected — i.e., cooler temperatures. This is the opposite of the expected warmer weather.",C:"HDD (Heating Degree Day) futures relate to heating demand (cold weather). Expecting warmer weather is BEARISH on HDD, not a reason to hedge with HDD.",D:"Selling a CDD call profits when temperatures are COOLER than expected — the opposite of the trader's view."}},
  {id:82,element:6,q:"Which of the following strategies is most speculative?",options:{A:"Long call, strike 100, and short asset position at 90",B:"Long call, strike 100, and short put strike 100",C:"Short put, strike 100, and long put strike 100",D:"Long put, strike 100, and a long asset at 110"},answer:"B",explanation:"Long call + short put at the same strike creates a synthetic long stock position with maximum leverage — unlimited upside AND substantial downside (short put loses as stock falls). No capital is deployed in the underlying, maximizing leverage. This is the most speculative of the four.",wrongExplanations:{A:"A long call + short asset is essentially a covered short or pairs trade — the short asset partially hedges the long call.",C:"Short put + long put at the same strike is a spread that nets close to zero — essentially flat with minimal risk or profit.",D:"Long put + long asset at $110 is a protective put — the long put hedges the downside of the long stock. This is a hedging strategy, not speculation."}},
  {id:83,element:6,q:"A Trader observes that a stock index futures contract is trading at $4,100, while the spot index is at $4,000. The annual risk-free rate is 5%, and there are 90 days to maturity. Ignoring dividends, what is the most appropriate action under fair value pricing?",options:{A:"Execute a cash-and-carry arbitrage by buying the index and selling the futures",B:"Ignore the pricing difference since the basis will remain unchanged until maturity",C:"Sell the index short and buy the futures to exploit a negative basis",D:"Hold the futures contract until it converges with intrinsic value at expiration"},answer:"A",explanation:"Fair value = $4,000 × (1 + 0.05 × 90/365) = $4,000 × 1.0123 = ~$4,049. Actual futures ($4,100) > fair value (~$4,049) = overpriced. Cash-and-carry: buy the spot index, sell the overpriced futures, lock in the ~$51 risk-free profit as they converge at expiry.",wrongExplanations:{B:"The basis does NOT remain unchanged — it converges to zero at expiry. Ignoring a mispricing means missing an arbitrage opportunity.",C:"Selling spot and buying futures is reverse cash-and-carry — used when futures are UNDERPRICED. Here they are overpriced, so the opposite is correct.",D:"Holding the futures alone doesn't exploit the mispricing — you need both legs (buy spot, sell futures) to lock in the arbitrage profit."}},
  {id:84,element:7,q:"A Registered Representative sees that a client's large intraday trades in a thin energy futures contract are affecting settlement prices. What is the correct immediate response?",options:{A:"Escalate as potential manipulation",B:"Permit trades if margin is sufficient",C:"Lower commissions to deter turnover",D:"Suggest trading on a larger venue"},answer:"A",explanation:"Trades that affect settlement prices constitute potential market manipulation — specifically artificial pricing, which is prohibited under UMIR. The RR must immediately escalate to compliance. The adequacy of margin is completely irrelevant to the manipulation concern.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Margin sufficiency does not authorize manipulative trading. A client with sufficient margin can still violate market integrity rules.",C:"Lowering commissions is a business response, not a regulatory compliance action. It does not address the potential manipulation.",D:"Suggesting a different venue does not resolve the market integrity concern — the escalation obligation exists regardless of venue choice."}},
  {id:85,element:2,q:"An Investment Dealer opens a futures contract account for a retail client. Which of the following best describes the regulatory requirement for the account application form?",options:{A:"Obtain a completed derivatives account application before the initial trade",B:"Obtain the derivatives account application within one workday after the initial trade",C:"Confirm the client's investment experience and risk tolerance on the application form",D:"Confirm that the client has completed a derivatives knowledge assessment prior to account approval"},answer:"A",explanation:"CIRO rules require that the completed derivatives account application form be obtained BEFORE the initial trade is executed. There is no grace period — the application must be completed and on file first.",wrongExplanations:{B:"There is no 'one workday after' grace period. The application must be obtained BEFORE the initial trade, not after.",C:"While investment experience and risk tolerance are collected, this describes the content of the form — not the timing requirement, which is the key point.",D:"A specific derivatives knowledge assessment is not a standard regulatory prerequisite — the application and risk disclosure are the required documents."}},
  {id:86,element:3,q:"An investor purchases a strike $60 call option on 100 shares of a stock with a premium of $5 per share. The stock's current price is $50 per share. What is the leverage ratio of this option position?",options:{A:"6:5, based on strike price and stock price",B:"10:1, based on market value and premium",C:"12:1, based on strike price and premium",D:"100:1, based on total shares per option"},answer:"B",explanation:"Leverage = Market Value of underlying controlled / Cost of option = (100 × $50) / (100 × $5) = $5,000 / $500 = 10:1.",wrongExplanations:{A:"6:5 is just the ratio of strike to stock price ($60/$50) — this is not the leverage ratio.",C:"12:1 uses the strike price ($60) instead of the market price ($50) in the numerator — the correct figure is the current market value.",D:"100:1 confuses the number of shares per contract (100) with the leverage ratio — these are unrelated calculations."}},
  {id:87,element:5,q:"When negotiating a commodity swap trade in the over-the-counter (OTC) market with a retail client, what action should an Investment Dealer take to ensure an accurate determination of the fair price?",options:{A:"Use the forward curve to calculate cash flows",B:"Utilize historical price movements of the underlying commodity",C:"Utilize aggregated quotes from multiple brokers",D:"Use the notional amount of the swap as the primary valuation metric"},answer:"A",explanation:"The forward curve (market expectations of future commodity prices across different time horizons) is the standard method for valuing commodity swap cash flows. It provides a market-based fair value for each payment date of the swap.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Historical price movements look backward — fair value requires forward-looking market prices (the forward curve), not historical prices.",C:"While broker quotes can provide useful reference points, the forward curve is the systematic, objective pricing approach required for fair valuation.",D:"The notional amount is the reference amount for calculating cash flows — it is not a valuation metric. Using it as a primary valuation metric would produce incorrect pricing."}},
  {id:88,element:2,q:"An Investment Dealer wishes to execute an over-the-counter (OTC) equity swap for a client. If there is no standard industry agreement or ISDA agreement in place, what must the Investment Dealer do to satisfy CIRO's requirement to document this?",options:{A:"Enter into an ISDA-recognized agreement with the client before executing the trade",B:"Record the details summarizing trade terms and archive it internally",C:"Update the monthly account statement to show the swap, with no separate confirmation",D:"Wait until the first variation-margin call posts, then ask the client to verify the original trade details"},answer:"A",explanation:"CIRO requires that OTC derivative transactions be documented under a recognized legal framework such as an ISDA Master Agreement BEFORE the trade is executed. Proper documentation is a prerequisite for OTC derivative trades, not an afterthought.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Internal archiving of trade terms is insufficient without the recognized legal agreement — a summary alone does not satisfy CIRO's OTC documentation requirement.",C:"Monthly statement updates are insufficient as documentation — an ISDA or equivalent agreement must be in place before trading begins.",D:"Waiting for the first variation-margin call before verifying trade details is dangerously late — documentation must precede execution."}},
  {id:89,element:5,q:"A Registered Representative is comparing two copper forward contracts, one priced from the London Metal Exchange (LME) daily cash price and the other from the three-month price on the Commodity Exchange (COMEX). Which variable must be aligned before the two hedges can be considered equivalent?",options:{A:"Tick size difference between the two exchanges",B:"Currency used for quotation on each exchange",C:"Storage fee charged by the warehouses for copper",D:"Minimum contract lot size that each market delivers"},answer:"D",explanation:"For two hedges to be functionally equivalent, the contract lot sizes (quantity of copper delivered per contract) must match. If one contract covers a different quantity than the other, they cannot be directly compared or combined — the hedge ratios would differ.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Tick size differences affect precision of entry/exit pricing but can be adjusted for — they don't prevent equivalence if lot sizes match.",B:"Currency differences can be hedged separately — while important, currency is not the primary variable needed to establish equivalence of the two copper hedges.",C:"Storage fees are part of cost-of-carry and affect fair value — but they don't determine whether two contracts can be considered equivalent hedges."}},
  {id:90,element:4,q:"A Registered Representative must price an exotic option with path-dependent knock-out barriers. Which modelling approach is most suitable?",options:{A:"Black-Scholes with analytic barrier tweaks",B:"One-step Binomial to capture early exercise",C:"Monte Carlo paths that track barrier hits",D:"Put-call parity formed by static hedges"},answer:"C",explanation:"Knock-out barrier options are path-dependent (the option ceases to exist if the underlying hits the barrier at any point during the option's life). Monte Carlo simulation generates thousands of possible price paths and can directly track whether each path triggers the barrier — making it the ideal model for path-dependent exotics.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Black-Scholes has analytic barrier solutions for simple cases, but complex path-dependent barriers require Monte Carlo for accuracy.",B:"A one-step Binomial is far too simple and cannot model path-dependent barrier features across continuous time.",D:"Put-call parity creates synthetic positions but cannot price path-dependent barrier options — it describes static relationships between vanilla options."}},
  {id:91,element:3,q:"An investor holds a call option with a strike price of 500 on equity asset A. The expiry date on the American-style option is in two months and equity asset A is currently trading at 580. Which of the following explanations indicate why the investor might not exercise the option now?",options:{A:"The option is not in the money and exercise would lead to a predictable loss",B:"If the equity issuer were scheduled to hold an important company meeting soon",C:"Selling to close the option instead is likely to generate a larger profit than exercising",D:"The underlying asset is physically delivered causing storage concerns"},answer:"C",explanation:"The option is deeply ITM (stock 580 > strike 500). However, selling the option in the market captures both intrinsic value AND remaining time value — always greater than exercising (which only captures intrinsic value). For a non-dividend stock, selling to close is almost always superior to early exercise.",wrongExplanations:{A:"The option IS in the money (stock 580 > strike 500) — intrinsic value = 80 points. This statement is factually incorrect.",B:"A company meeting might be a consideration for timing stock trades, but it is not a standard reason to delay option exercise — and it's not the best answer here.",D:"Equity options are cash-settled or settled with stock delivery — not physical commodity storage. Storage concerns apply to commodity options, not equity options."}},
  {id:92,element:5,q:"Under Canadian Depository for Securities (CDS) rules, who may file a buy-in notice when securities due on the settlement date are not delivered?",options:{A:"The delivering participant that is short the shares",B:"The receiving participant that is still awaiting delivery",C:"The staff of CDS Clearing Risk Management on behalf of the parties",D:"The issuer's transfer agent, acting for the registered shareholders"},answer:"B",explanation:"Under CDS rules, the receiving participant — the party that is owed the securities and has not received them — has the right to file a buy-in notice against the failing delivering participant to force settlement.",wrongExplanations:{A:"The delivering participant is the party that failed to deliver — they would not file a buy-in notice against themselves.",C:"CDS Clearing Risk Management oversees systemic risk but does not file buy-in notices on behalf of participants — this is the receiving participant's right.",D:"Transfer agents register securities ownership but have no role in CDS settlement buy-in procedures."}},
  {id:93,element:4,q:"A non-dividend-paying stock trades at $60.00. The risk-free rate is 2% per year (simple interest). A three-month European call with a $65.00 strike costs $1.05. According to put-call parity, what is the minimum fair value of the matching put (nearest cent)?",options:{A:"$5.28",B:"$5.73",C:"$6.22",D:"$6.78"},answer:"B",explanation:"Put-call parity: P = C - S + PV(K). PV(K) = $65 / (1 + 0.02 × 3/12) = $65 / 1.005 = $64.677. P = $1.05 - $60.00 + $64.677 = $5.727 ≈ $5.73.",wrongExplanations:{A:"$5.28 uses an incorrect PV calculation — perhaps applying the rate differently.",C:"$6.22 overstates the PV of the strike or makes an arithmetic error.",D:"$6.78 significantly overstates the result — likely a calculation error in discounting."}},
  {id:94,element:3,q:"An investor sells uncovered (naked) call options on a volatile stock. What is the primary risk/reward trade-off?",options:{A:"Limited premium income versus limited potential losses",B:"Limited premium income versus potentially unlimited losses",C:"Potentially unlimited gains versus limited investment loss",D:"Limited potential gains versus limited investment loss"},answer:"B",explanation:"Short naked calls: maximum profit = premium received (limited). If the stock price rises sharply, losses are theoretically unlimited (no ceiling on how high the stock can go). This asymmetric risk — limited upside, unlimited downside — is the defining risk/reward of naked call writing.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Losses are NOT limited for naked calls — they are potentially unlimited as the stock price can rise indefinitely.",C:"Potentially unlimited gains describes a LONG option or long stock position, not a short naked call.",D:"Limited gains AND limited losses describes a short put or a spread — not a naked call where losses are unlimited."}},
  {id:95,element:3,q:"A futures Trader pays $10 commission for each round-trip trade (entry + exit included). If the Trader executes 75 round-trip trades in one month, what are their explicit transaction costs?",options:{A:"The Trader pays $750 in commissions",B:"The transaction cost is capped at $500 by exchange policy",C:"The Trader incurs $1,500 in total trading costs",D:"The transaction cost is $7,500 before netting adjustments"},answer:"A",explanation:"Explicit transaction costs = number of trades × cost per round-trip = 75 × $10 = $750. Since $10 per round-trip includes both entry and exit, there is no additional multiplication needed.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Exchange policies do not cap commission costs at $500. There is no such exchange-imposed cap.",C:"$1,500 would result from multiplying $10 by 150 (double-counting each leg). The question specifies $10 per ROUND-TRIP — both legs are already included.",D:"$7,500 = $10 × 750 — this would be 750 round-trip trades, not 75."}},
  {id:96,element:4,q:"A Registered Representative reviews quarterly crude oil supply-demand figures, OPEC production quotas and refinery-capacity data to forecast prices. Which forecasting approach is being applied?",options:{A:"Momentum divergence analysis",B:"Fundamental analysis",C:"Fibonacci retracement analysis",D:"Moving-average crossover analysis"},answer:"B",explanation:"Fundamental analysis uses supply and demand data, production figures (OPEC quotas), capacity data, and economic fundamentals to forecast prices. All of the described data sources are classic fundamental inputs for commodity price forecasting.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Momentum divergence analysis uses price and momentum indicators from charts — not supply-demand or production data.",C:"Fibonacci retracement analysis uses mathematical ratios derived from historical price movements — a technical analysis tool.",D:"Moving-average crossover analysis uses historical price averages to generate signals — a technical, not fundamental, approach."}},
  {id:97,element:4,q:"Which data point is most relevant when preparing a fundamental price forecast for natural gas futures?",options:{A:"Relative Strength Index dropping under 30 on the chart",B:"Rising storage levels on US Energy Information Administration report",C:"Fifty-day simple moving average turning upward last week",D:"Bollinger Band Width contracting to its narrowest range"},answer:"B",explanation:"Rising storage levels (from EIA reports) are a fundamental supply indicator for natural gas. More supply in storage typically leads to lower prices. EIA storage reports are the most closely watched fundamental data point in natural gas markets.",wrongExplanations:{A:"RSI below 30 is a technical indicator suggesting oversold conditions — not a fundamental data point.",C:"A 50-day moving average is a price-based technical analysis tool — not fundamental data.",D:"Bollinger Band Width is a volatility measure from technical analysis — not a fundamental supply-demand input."}},
  {id:98,element:4,q:"Call and put options (both with a $60 strike and six months to expiry) are priced at $5.20 and $3.00, respectively. The stock is $62, interest rate is 0%. What should an arbitrager do to make a profit?",options:{A:"Put-call parity holds; no arbitrage opportunity exists",B:"Put is overpriced; sell put and buy stock and buy the call",C:"Options are mispriced; arbitrage by shorting call, buying put and buying stock",D:"The zero interest rate makes it impossible to make a profit"},answer:"C",explanation:"At 0% rate, put-call parity: C - P = S - K = $62 - $60 = $2. Observed: C - P = $5.20 - $3.00 = $2.20. LHS ($2.20) > RHS ($2.00): call is overpriced. Arbitrage: short the overpriced call, buy the put, buy the stock. This portfolio = synthetic short call vs actual short call — locks in $0.20 risk-free profit.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Parity doesn't hold: C - P = $2.20 but S - K = $2.00. The $0.20 difference is an arbitrage opportunity.",B:"It is the CALL that is overpriced, not the put. If the put were overpriced, you'd sell it — but buying a put when the call is overpriced is wrong.",D:"Zero interest rate simplifies the parity calculation (PV(K) = K) — it does not prevent arbitrage. The mispricing creates the opportunity."}},
  {id:99,element:6,q:"An energy company observes June natural gas futures trading $0.12 below their theoretical cash-and-carry value after financing and storage. Which arbitrage trade should the Registered Representative recommend?",options:{A:"Buy futures, sell physical gas",B:"Sell futures, buy physical gas",C:"Sell futures, sell physical gas",D:"Buy futures, buy physical gas"},answer:"A",explanation:"Futures are UNDERPRICED vs fair value (below cash-and-carry cost). When futures are cheap: buy the cheap futures and sell the expensive physical gas. This reverse cash-and-carry strategy locks in a risk-free profit as prices converge.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Sell futures + buy physical is standard cash-and-carry — used when futures are OVERPRICED. Here they are underpriced, so the reverse applies.",C:"Selling both futures and physical creates an outright short position — not an arbitrage strategy.",D:"Buying both futures and physical creates an outright long position — not a risk-free arbitrage strategy."}},
  {id:100,element:4,q:"An analyst is pricing an option on a stock paying regular dividends. Which factor limits the basic Black-Scholes model's accuracy in this scenario?",options:{A:"Assumption that dividends increase stock prices",B:"Assumption of variable dividend payments",C:"Requirement for constant dividend yields",D:"Omission of dividend payments"},answer:"D",explanation:"The basic Black-Scholes model was designed for non-dividend-paying stocks. It omits the effect of dividend payments, which reduce the stock price on the ex-dividend date. This omission makes it inaccurate for dividend-paying stocks.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Black-Scholes does not assume dividends increase stock prices — this is fabricated. Dividends reduce stock prices.",B:"Black-Scholes doesn't assume variable dividends — it simply omits dividends entirely in its basic form.",C:"Some extensions of Black-Scholes do assume constant dividend yield as an approximation. But the basic model's limitation is that it OMITS dividends entirely."}},
  {id:101,element:4,q:"A call option on a Canadian stock has a strike price of $50, the stock price is $54 and the option premium is $5. Using basic pricing theory, what is the time value of this option?",options:{A:"$0",B:"$1",C:"$4",D:"$5"},answer:"B",explanation:"Intrinsic value = MAX($54 - $50, 0) = $4. Time value = Premium - Intrinsic value = $5 - $4 = $1.",wrongExplanations:{A:"$0 would mean the entire premium is intrinsic value — but $4 is intrinsic and $1 is time value, so time value is not zero.",C:"$4 is the INTRINSIC value, not the time value. Time value = Premium ($5) - Intrinsic ($4) = $1.",D:"$5 is the total premium — this would be the time value only if the option had zero intrinsic value (i.e., if it were OTM). This option is ITM."}},
  {id:102,element:7,q:"An Investment Dealer's proprietary desk trades ahead of a pending client block trade in the same contract. In what circumstance is this permitted?",options:{A:"The client is informed and consents",B:"The proprietary trade is for hedging purposes",C:"The Investment Dealer reports the trade to CIRO within 24 hours",D:"The client's order is executed within five minutes"},answer:"A",explanation:"Frontrunning is prohibited under UMIR. The ONLY circumstance where trading ahead of a client order may be permissible is if the client has been fully informed and has given explicit prior consent. Client consent is the key distinguishing factor.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Hedging purposes do not exempt the Investment Dealer from frontrunning prohibitions. The client must consent.",C:"Reporting to CIRO after the fact does not make frontrunning permissible — the violation occurs at the time of trading, not when it is disclosed.",D:"Executing the client order within five minutes does not retroactively make the proprietary order permissible. Client consent is required."}},
  {id:103,element:6,q:"A Trader notices a strong contango in the crude oil futures market. The Trader expects this price gap to narrow over time. Which of the following strategies would best profit from this expectation?",options:{A:"Long both near-term and far-term crude oil futures",B:"Short both near-term and far-term crude oil futures",C:"Long near-term and short far-term crude oil futures",D:"Short near-term and long far-term crude oil futures"},answer:"C",explanation:"Contango: far-dated futures priced higher than near-term. Expecting the spread to NARROW: buy the cheaper near-term and sell the expensive far-term. If contango narrows, the near-term gains more (or falls less) than the far-term, generating a profit on the spread.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Going long both contracts is a directional bet on oil prices rising — not a spread strategy to profit from contango narrowing.",B:"Going short both is a directional bet on oil prices falling — also not a spread strategy.",D:"Short near-term + long far-term bets on contango WIDENING (far-term increasing relative to near-term) — the opposite of the expectation."}},
  {id:104,element:3,q:"Two counterparties enter into an over-the-counter (OTC) swap agreement worth $10 million. One party defaults after 70% of the notional is accrued. What is the exposure faced by the other party?",options:{A:"$7 million, representing the accrued value at risk from counterparty default",B:"$3 million, and posted margin can be recovered",C:"Limited to the cost of securing a replacement contract",D:"$10 million, since no collateral is needed to mitigate losses"},answer:"A",explanation:"The defaulting party has accrued 70% of the $10 million notional = $7 million of accrued value at risk. The non-defaulting party faces potential loss of this $7 million in the event of the counterparty default on the OTC swap.",wrongExplanations:{B:"$3 million represents the remaining unaccrued portion. The exposure is to what has already accrued, not what remains.",C:"While replacement cost is a factor in OTC credit exposure, the question asks about the amount at risk — which is the accrued value ($7 million).",D:"$10 million would be the total exposure if the entire notional were at risk. Here, 70% has accrued, so exposure is $7 million."}},
  {id:105,element:1,q:"A Registered Representative is working with a commercial agricultural client who uses derivatives to manage crop price risk. The client's trading volume has increased significantly, and the RR sees that the client now meets the institutional client threshold. Which action must the RR take to remain compliant?",options:{A:"Update the client's KYC documentation and notify the client of the institutional classification in writing",B:"Increase the client's margin requirements to institutional standards and backdate the changes",C:"File a hedger status exemption with the derivatives exchange and suspend trading until approved",D:"Assume institutional classification applies automatically and begin sending standard institutional reports only"},answer:"A",explanation:"When a client's status changes to institutional, the RR must update the KYC documentation to reflect the new classification AND formally notify the client in writing that they are now classified as an institutional client. This process cannot be automatic or assumed.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Backdating changes to compliance records is not permissible — changes must be made prospectively. Also, margin requirements may differ from retail, not necessarily increase.",C:"Filing exemptions with the exchange is not the required step when a client meets the institutional threshold through volume.",D:"Institutional classification cannot be assumed automatically — it requires formal documentation, KYC update, and written notification to the client."}},
  {id:106,element:2,q:"A retail client opens accounts to trade equity options and commodity futures. Which of the following must be provided by the Investment Dealer?",options:{A:"A margin account agreement",B:"A letter of undertaking",C:"A suitability waiver",D:"A derivatives risk disclosure statement"},answer:"D",explanation:"A Derivatives Risk Disclosure Statement must be provided to any client before they begin trading derivatives — options, futures, or both. This is a mandatory regulatory requirement for all derivatives accounts under CIRO rules.",wrongExplanations:{A:"A margin agreement is required if the client is borrowing funds on margin — but the FIRST mandatory document for derivatives is the risk disclosure statement.",B:"A letter of undertaking is not a standard derivatives account requirement.",C:"Suitability waivers are not standard regulatory documents — dealers must perform suitability assessments, not waive them."}},
  {id:107,element:2,q:"When must an Investment Dealer send a retail client their performance report and fee/charge report?",options:{A:"At the same time the monthly account statement is sent",B:"Within 10 business days after the end of the month",C:"Within 10 days after the account statement for the same period",D:"On a quarterly basis, together with the quarterly account statement"},answer:"C",explanation:"CIRO rules require performance reports and fee/charge reports to be sent within 10 days after the account statement for the same period. They follow (not accompany) the account statement.",wrongExplanations:{A:"They are not sent simultaneously with the monthly statement — they follow it within 10 days.",B:"The 10-day clock starts after the account statement is sent, not after month-end. The reference point is the statement date.",D:"These reports are required to accompany or follow ANNUAL statements (not quarterly). For the quarterly period, only account statements are required."}},
  {id:108,element:3,q:"A portfolio uses S&P 500 futures with a notional value of $500,000 and a $25,000 margin deposit. If the index rises 4%, what is the portfolio return?",options:{A:"4%","B":"20%",C:"80%",D:"100%"},answer:"C",explanation:"Gain on futures = 4% × $500,000 = $20,000. Return on margin capital = $20,000 / $25,000 = 80%. This demonstrates how leverage dramatically amplifies returns — a 4% market move produces an 80% return on the margin posted.",wrongExplanations:{A:"4% would be the return if you held the underlying directly with no leverage — not a leveraged futures position.",B:"20% would result from dividing the return by the notional ($20,000/$100,000) — not the correct denominator. Return is measured against capital deployed (margin).",D:"100% return would require the gain to equal the margin ($25,000 gain / $25,000 margin) — a 5% index move, not 4%."}},
  {id:109,element:6,q:"An investor holds a long position in a stock and uses put options to hedge downside risk. Which outcome is most consistent with an imperfect hedge?",options:{A:"The stock price increases and the put hedge generates excess gains",B:"The stock price declines and the put hedge does not fully offset losses",C:"The stock price increases and the investor exercises puts above market price",D:"The stock price declines and the puts expire worthless, increasing returns"},answer:"B",explanation:"An imperfect hedge means the hedge does not fully neutralize the risk. If the stock falls and the put hedge only partially offsets the loss (due to basis risk, wrong strike, or insufficient contracts), residual losses remain. This is the classic definition of an imperfect hedge.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Put options create asymmetric protection — they do not generate 'excess gains' when the stock rises. When stock rises, puts expire worthless (a cost, not a gain).",C:"An investor would not exercise puts above market price — puts are exercised when the stock is BELOW the strike. This scenario is impossible.",D:"If the stock declines and puts expire worthless, the hedge failed entirely — this is worse than an imperfect hedge; it's a completely ineffective one."}},
  {id:110,element:5,q:"An Investment Dealer's risk management system flags a client's account for exceeding margin limits. The client holds: Long 50 gold futures (initial margin $5,000 per contract) and Short 30 silver futures (initial margin $3,500 per contract). The Investment Dealer's policy requires total margin coverage of 110% for excess concentration. What is the additional margin deposit the client must post to comply?",options:{A:"$35,500",B:"$55,000",C:"$93,500",D:"$137,500"},answer:"A",explanation:"Base margin: (50 × $5,000) + (30 × $3,500) = $250,000 + $105,000 = $355,000. Required at 110% = $355,000 × 1.10 = $390,500. Additional margin needed = $390,500 - $355,000 = $35,500.",wrongExplanations:{B:"$55,000 might result from multiplying $355,000 by 10% incorrectly (getting $35,500 right but then doubling it).",C:"$93,500 is not derived from a correct application of the 110% rule to the base margin.",D:"$137,500 appears to be the base margin multiplied by incorrect factors."}},
  {id:111,element:5,q:"An institutional client contacts a derivatives sales desk at an Investment Dealer about hedging their exposure to Canadian dollar (CAD) and US dollar (USD) exchange rate movements. What is the primary role of the sell-side trading desk in this case?",options:{A:"Take a principal position in a CAD/USD forward contract to manage the client's currency risk",B:"Execute a CAD/USD forward contract on an agency basis and charge a commission",C:"Provide pricing, liquidity and tailored CAD/USD hedge solutions using the Investment Dealer's capital",D:"Analyze the client's exposures and suggest CAD/USD option strategies for protection"},answer:"C",explanation:"The sell-side trading desk's primary role is to provide pricing, liquidity, and tailored derivative solutions to institutional clients. They typically act as principal — using the firm's own capital to take the other side of the trade — and earn the bid-ask spread.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Taking a principal position is part of the answer but incomplete — the primary role also includes providing pricing, liquidity, and tailored solutions, not just position-taking.",B:"Agency execution (charging a commission) describes a brokerage function, not typical sell-side derivatives dealer activity. Dealers typically act as principal.",D:"Analyzing exposures and suggesting strategies is an advisory function — while valuable, it is not the PRIMARY role of the trading desk, which is to provide liquidity and execute."}},
  {id:112,element:3,q:"Why do speculators favour futures contracts over forward contracts for trading?",options:{A:"Futures offer customizable contract terms",B:"Futures require lower margin deposits",C:"Futures provide liquidity through exchanges",D:"Futures eliminate counterparty default risk"},answer:"C",explanation:"Speculators prefer futures because they are exchange-traded and therefore highly liquid — positions can be entered and exited quickly at transparent market prices. Forward contracts are OTC, illiquid, and customized — making them difficult for speculators to exit quickly.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Customizable terms are an advantage of FORWARDS, not futures. Futures are standardized.",B:"Margin deposit comparisons vary and are not the primary reason speculators prefer futures. Liquidity is the key driver.",D:"While futures do reduce counterparty risk through clearing houses, this is more important to hedgers and risk managers than to speculators whose primary concern is liquidity."}},
  {id:113,element:2,q:"An Investment Dealer executes an equity index futures trade for a client. Which of the following are required to be recorded in the daily Trade Blotter?",options:{A:"Delivery date, account balance, margin requirement",B:"Futures price, broker commission, settlement fees",C:"Underlying interest, quantity, price, transaction dates",D:"Multiplier, tick size, settlement convention"},answer:"C",explanation:"The core elements required in the Trade Blotter for derivatives include: the underlying interest (what is being traded), quantity (number of contracts), price (execution price), and transaction dates. These are the minimum required trade records.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Account balance and margin requirements are account-level records, not trade-specific blotter entries. Delivery date may be included but is not the primary answer.",B:"Broker commission and settlement fees are cost records — important but not the primary trade blotter requirements specified under CIRO rules.",D:"Multiplier, tick size, and settlement convention are contract specification data — not the specific trade-execution records required in the blotter."}},
  {id:114,element:8,q:"A derivatives Registered Representative helps a client generate a return of $85,000 on a structured product. The client, who owns a chain of vacation properties, offers the RR a free three-night stay valued at $2,100. The RR believes this gesture is unrelated to performance and would not impact their objectivity. What is the most appropriate action for the RR to take?",options:{A:"Accept the offer only after receiving written approval from compliance",B:"Decline the offer unless the client's investment committee provides direct approval in writing",C:"Accept the offer if it complies with the Investment Dealer's hospitality threshold and is unrelated to performance",D:"Decline the offer outright, as receiving benefits from a client creates an automatic conflict"},answer:"D",explanation:"Regardless of the RR's belief about the gesture's intent, accepting significant personal benefits from clients creates conflicts of interest. CIRO standards prohibit accepting any consideration that could compromise professional independence. A $2,100 stay is material and should be declined.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Compliance approval does not override the prohibition on accepting material benefits from clients in circumstances that create conflicts of interest.",B:"The client's investment committee approval is not relevant — the issue is the professional standards obligation of the RR, not the client's authorization.",C:"Even if within an hospitality threshold, accepting vacation accommodations from a client whose account you manage creates a conflict that should be avoided."}},
  {id:115,element:2,q:"Which of the following statements best reflects the appropriate actions of a derivatives Investment Dealer in relation to client assets?",options:{A:"Keep client assets separate from Investment Dealer funds in case of liquidation",B:"Use client assets for Investment Dealer proprietary trading to enhance returns",C:"Combine client assets with Investment Dealer operating funds to cover counterparty risk",D:"Invest client assets in Investment Dealer managed funds to provide diversification"},answer:"A",explanation:"Segregation of client assets is a fundamental regulatory requirement. Client assets must be kept completely separate from the Investment Dealer's own funds. This protects clients in the event the dealer becomes insolvent.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Using client assets for proprietary trading is strictly prohibited — this would constitute misappropriation of client funds.",C:"Combining client and firm assets is prohibited regardless of the justification — segregation is a non-negotiable regulatory requirement.",D:"Investing client assets in firm-managed funds without client instruction and consent constitutes a conflict of interest and unauthorized use of client assets."}},
  {id:116,element:4,q:"Which feature makes a Binomial lattice preferable to the Black-Scholes formula when valuing an employee stock option with a vesting schedule?",options:{A:"The lattice can embed date-based exercise restrictions and test early exercise at every node",B:"The lattice forces one exercise date for all nodes but uses different discount rates across the tree",C:"The lattice values the option as European by allowing exercise only at the final step of the tree",D:"The lattice assumes exercise can occur continuously and therefore collapses to a single closed-form solution"},answer:"A",explanation:"A Binomial lattice can incorporate date-specific exercise restrictions (like vesting dates) by disabling exercise at pre-vesting nodes, and then test for optimal early exercise at every permitted node after vesting. This flexibility is impossible in Black-Scholes.",wrongExplanations:{B:"The lattice does not force one exercise date — it can allow exercise at any node after vesting, which is the advantage.",C:"Restricting exercise to the final step makes it a European pricing approach — losing the American/early-exercise advantage that makes the lattice valuable here.",D:"Black-Scholes assumes continuous exercise (European) and collapses to a closed form — the Binomial does not make this assumption."}},
  {id:117,element:4,q:"An investor is reviewing the fair value of a call option on a publicly traded stock. Which of the following best explains how the price of the underlying asset impacts the option's price?",options:{A:"It determines the direction of volatility in the derivative contract",B:"It affects the intrinsic value and the time value of the option",C:"It provides a general forecast of the option's strike movement",D:"It influences only the premium paid, not the derivative's value"},answer:"B",explanation:"The underlying asset price directly affects both intrinsic value (MAX(S-K, 0) for a call) AND time value (through its relationship to the strike — whether the option is ITM, ATM, or OTM determines time value). Both components of the premium are impacted.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Volatility direction is determined by market supply-demand dynamics, not by the price level of the underlying asset itself.",C:"The underlying price does not forecast strike movement — strikes are fixed at the time the option is written.",D:"The underlying price affects the full premium value — both intrinsic and time value components. Saying it only affects the 'premium paid' mischaracterizes the relationship."}},
  {id:118,element:6,q:"A farmer sells futures to hedge against crop price declines. Assuming the hedge totally covers the underlying asset, what is the primary risk of this hedging strategy?",options:{A:"Margin call risk",B:"Liquidity risk",C:"Price risk",D:"Basis risk"},answer:"D",explanation:"When a hedge fully covers the position, outright price risk is largely eliminated. The remaining primary risk is basis risk — the risk that the spot price and futures price do not move in perfect synchrony due to differences in location, grade, delivery timing, or quality specifications.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Margin calls are a cash flow risk (operational), not the PRIMARY risk of the hedging strategy itself — and can be managed with cash reserves.",B:"Liquidity risk exists but the question specifies a fully-covered position, suggesting adequate contract availability. Basis risk is the more primary concern.",C:"Price risk is the risk the hedge is designed to ELIMINATE. Once fully covered, outright price risk is minimal — basis risk takes over as the primary concern."}},
  {id:119,element:5,q:"When a Canadian Derivatives Clearing Corporation (CDCC) clearing member fails to meet a variation-margin call by the deadline, which action is taken first in the default management sequence?",options:{A:"Declare the member in default, issue formal notice to regulators and suspend clearing access",B:"Draw on the mutualized guaranty fund to cover the unpaid margin",C:"Port all of the member's client positions to another solvent clearing participant",D:"File a claim against the member's private insurance policy to recover the unpaid margin"},answer:"A",explanation:"The first step in CDCC's default management sequence is to formally declare the member in default, notify relevant regulators, and suspend their clearing access. Only after this formal declaration would the clearing house proceed to use the member's own resources and then the guaranty fund.",wrongExplanations:{B:"Drawing on the guaranty fund comes AFTER the member's own resources (initial margin, default fund contribution) are exhausted — not as the first action.",C:"Porting client positions is a client protection measure that may occur during default management but is not the FIRST step — declaration of default comes first.",D:"Insurance claims are not part of CDCC's defined default management sequence."}},
  {id:120,element:5,q:"A farmer is long one corn futures contract that requires an initial margin of $4,400 and a maintenance margin of $4,000. After several days, the account equity has fallen to $3,650. The Registered Representative wants to reduce risk and avoid a margin call. Which action meets the exchange margin rules?",options:{A:"Pay in $350 to raise equity back to maintenance margin",B:"Deposit $750 to raise equity back to initial margin",C:"Sell a corn call option to collect $400 of premium",D:"Reduce the corn futures position so equity exceeds maintenance"},answer:"D",explanation:"When equity falls below maintenance margin ($4,000), a margin call requires restoration to the INITIAL margin ($4,400). However, the RR wants to avoid a margin call and reduce risk. Reducing (closing) the futures position decreases the required margin — if fewer contracts are held, the remaining equity may exceed maintenance margin for the reduced position.",wrongExplanations:{A:"Paying $350 only restores equity to $4,000 (maintenance level) — exchange rules require restoration to INITIAL margin ($4,400), not just maintenance. This would not satisfy a margin call.",B:"While $750 does restore equity to initial margin ($4,400), it increases capital at risk — it doesn't REDUCE risk as required.",C:"Selling a call option introduces a new obligation and does not directly address the futures margin shortfall in the account."}},
];


